window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"topics/agent-sdk.html","title":"Agent SDK","definition":"The Claude Agent SDK is a development kit that gives programmers the same tools, agent loop, and context management that power Claude Code — programmable in Python and TypeScript to build custom AI agents .","headings":["What is the Agent SDK?","Built-in Capabilities","Use Cases"],"text":"Agent SDK The Claude Agent SDK is a development kit that gives programmers the same tools, agent loop, and context management that power Claude Code — programmable in Python and TypeScript to build custom AI agents . What is the Agent SDK? An SDK (Software Development Kit) is a collection of tools and libraries that helps developers build applications. The Claude Agent SDK specifically helps developers build AI agents — autonomous programs that can read files, run commands, edit code, browse the web, and complete multi-step tasks. Instead of building all the infrastructure from scratch (tool execution, conversation management, safety checks), the Agent SDK provides these as ready-to-use building blocks. You focus on defining what your agent should do; the SDK handles how it runs. Built-in Capabilities File reading, writing, and searching Running bash commands Code editing Web browsing MCP server connections Conversation and context management Use Cases Developers use the Agent SDK to build custom automation: code review bots, data analysis pipelines, content generation systems, CI/CD agents that fix failing tests, customer support agents, and any workflow that benefits from an AI that can think and act autonomously. Layman's Analogy If Claude Code is a fully assembled robot that helps you code, the Agent SDK is the robot parts kit — motors, sensors, circuit boards — that lets you build your own custom robot for whatever job you need. You get all the same powerful components, but you decide how they're assembled."},{"url":"topics/ai-agent.html","title":"AI Agent","definition":"An AI agent is an AI system that can autonomously plan and execute multi-step tasks — not just answer questions, but take actions in the world to achieve goals, dynamically deciding its own path.","headings":["What is an AI Agent?","Agent vs Workflow: When to Use Which","Agent Components","Single Agent vs Multi-Agent","Examples"],"text":"AI Agent An AI agent is an AI system that can autonomously plan and execute multi-step tasks — not just answer questions, but take actions in the world to achieve goals, dynamically deciding its own path. What is an AI Agent? A regular chatbot responds to one message at a time. An AI agent goes further: given a goal, it breaks it down into steps, decides which tools to use, executes actions, observes results, and adjusts its plan — all with minimal human supervision. For example, if you tell an agent \"book me a flight to London next Tuesday under $500,\" it would search flights, compare options, fill in your details, and complete the booking — making decisions along the way. Agent vs Workflow: When to Use Which The key distinction: in an agent, the model decides the path . In a workflow , you define the path . Use a workflow when the steps are predictable and the same every time (translating reviews, classifying emails, generating reports) Use an agent when the task requires ongoing judgment, the path is unknown upfront, or you need adaptive problem-solving When agents pay off: Imagine the task is \"investigate why customer complaints spiked this week.\" The agent might query the database, notice a pattern in shipping delays, check the logistics provider's status page, cross-reference with a recent deployment, and read related Slack messages. You cannot predefine these steps because you don't know where the data will lead. That's when you need an agent. Agent Components Brain: An LLM that reasons and makes decisions Tools: Capabilities like web browsing, file access, code execution (often via MCP ) — see Tool Use Memory: Ability to remember context across steps Planning: Breaking goals into sub-tasks Tool-Use Loop: Observe → Think → Act → Get Result → Repeat until goal is achieved (see Tool Use for details) Single Agent vs Multi-Agent Most production agents are single agents with many tools . One LLM with access to search, databases, file systems, and APIs can handle remarkably complex tasks. Multi-agent systems add coordination overhead — only use them when a single agent's context window is overwhelmed, when subtasks need different permission levels, or when you need distinct expertise domains that would conflict in one system prompt. Examples Claude Code is an AI agent for software development. It reads code, plans changes, edits files, runs tests, and commits — all autonomously, deciding what to do based on what it finds. Other examples include customer support agents, research agents, and data analysis agents built with the Agent SDK . Layman's Analogy A regular AI is like a librarian — you ask a question, they give you an answer. An AI agent is like a personal assistant — you say \"plan my vacation,\" and they research destinations, compare prices, book hotels, arrange transport, and send you the itinerary. They don't just inform; they act. And crucially, they decide the order themselves based on what they find — they don't follow a script."},{"url":"topics/ai-automation.html","title":"AI Automation","definition":"AI Automation is the umbrella term for using AI to perform tasks without human intervention — spanning from simple prompt chains and predetermined workflows to fully autonomous AI agents . It encompasses both the patterns (how work flows) and the platforms (where you build it).","headings":["What is AI Automation?","The Automation Spectrum","The Big Three Platforms (2026)","Which to Choose?","AI Automation + Agent SDK"],"text":"AI Automation AI Automation is the umbrella term for using AI to perform tasks without human intervention — spanning from simple prompt chains and predetermined workflows to fully autonomous AI agents . It encompasses both the patterns (how work flows) and the platforms (where you build it). What is AI Automation? Traditional automation follows rigid rules: \"When an email arrives, save the attachment.\" AI automation adds intelligence: \"When an email arrives, read it, classify its intent, extract key data, update the CRM, and draft a response.\" By combining LLMs with tool use and workflow platforms, you create systems that handle complex, judgment-based tasks autonomously. In 2026, AI automation platforms are the primary way non-developers build AI agents and connect AI to their business tools — without writing code. The Automation Spectrum AI automation is not one thing — it's a spectrum from simple to complex: Single prompt: One LLM call solves the task (\"Summarize this email\") Prompt chain: Fixed sequence of LLM calls, output feeds into next (\"Translate → Classify → Summarize\") Workflow : Predetermined multi-step pipeline with routing , parallelization, and human checkpoints Agent : The model dynamically decides its own path, using tools in a loop until the goal is achieved Anthropic's guidance: Start with the simplest solution that works. A single well-crafted prompt often beats a complex workflow. A workflow often beats an agent. Only add complexity when simpler approaches genuinely fail. Most production \"AI agents\" are actually workflows — and that's correct. The Big Three Platforms (2026) n8n — The Developer's Choice n8n is the consensus leader for AI-native automation in 2026, especially for technical teams and cloud ecosystems. It's a fair-code, self-hostable platform with native AI agent capabilities. AI-native: ~70 dedicated AI nodes, built-in LangChain integration, multi-agent orchestration, RAG support Self-hostable: Run on your own infrastructure (AWS, GCP, Azure) for full data control — or use their cloud MCP support: Connect AI agents to any MCP-compatible tool Pricing: Free Community Edition (unlimited executions self-hosted); cloud plans charge per execution, not per step — so a 200-step workflow costs the same as a 2-step one 500+ integrations plus custom HTTP/webhook nodes for anything with an API n8n is ideal when you need: full control over your data, complex multi-agent workflows, or you're working within a cloud ecosystem (AWS/GCP/Azure) and want to self-host. Make (formerly Integromat) — Visual Power User Make offers a visual canvas-based workflow builder that sits between n8n's technical flexibility and Zapier's simplicity. Its branching and parallel processing capabilities are excellent for complex workflows. Visual canvas: Drag-and-drop workflow design with branching paths Generous free tier: 1,000 operations/month with two active scenarios Best for: Teams that want visual workflow design without self-hosting Cloud-only: No self-hosting option Zapier — The Easiest Start Zapier is the largest automation platform (8,000+ app integrations) and the easiest to use. In 2026, it repositioned as an \"AI orchestration platform\" for enterprise teams. Easiest to learn: No technical knowledge required Most integrations: 8,000+ pre-built app connections Best for: Non-technical teams, quick simple automations Downside: Most expensive at scale; charges per task (each step counts) Which to Choose? Criteria n8n Make Zapier AI capabilities Best (native agents, RAG, MCP) Good Good Self-hosting Yes (free) No No Ease of use Technical Moderate Easiest Cost at scale Cheapest Mid-range Most expensive Integrations 500+ 1,500+ 8,000+ Cloud ecosystem fit Best (deploy anywhere) Good (cloud-only) Good (cloud-only) AI Automation + Agent SDK For developers, the Claude Agent SDK provides programmatic agent-building in Python/TypeScript — more power, more control, but requires coding. Automation platforms like n8n let you build similar agents visually. Many teams use both: Agent SDK for custom production agents, n8n for internal workflows and prototyping. Layman's Analogy AI automation is like hiring a team of invisible assistants who work 24/7. You set up the instructions once (\"when a customer emails, classify it, route it to the right department, and draft a reply\"), and the system handles it forever — learning from context the way a smart employee would, not just following a script. Real-World Example (n8n) A sales team sets up an n8n workflow: when a new lead fills out a form → Claude analyzes the company's website → enriches the CRM record → scores the lead → if high-priority, drafts a personalized outreach email and schedules it → if low-priority, adds to nurture sequence. Zero manual work after setup."},{"url":"topics/ai-model.html","title":"AI Model","definition":"An AI model is a mathematical system trained on data to recognize patterns and make predictions or generate outputs — the \"brain\" inside an AI application.","headings":["What is an AI Model?","Types of Models","Model vs. Product"],"text":"AI Model An AI model is a mathematical system trained on data to recognize patterns and make predictions or generate outputs — the \"brain\" inside an AI application. What is an AI Model? An AI model is the result of the machine learning process. You feed training data into an algorithm, and what comes out is a model — a file containing billions of learned parameters (numbers) that encode patterns from the data. When you give this model new input, it uses those patterns to produce useful output. Claude is an AI model. So is GPT-4, Gemini, DALL-E, and Stable Diffusion. Each is trained differently, on different data, for different tasks. Types of Models Language models : Generate and understand text (Claude, GPT) Image models: Generate or classify images (DALL-E, Midjourney) Audio models: Transcribe speech, generate music Multimodal models: Handle multiple types — text, images, audio Model vs. Product The model is the engine; the product is the car. Claude the model is the neural network with its trained weights. Claude the product includes the chat interface, safety systems, tool use, MCP connections, and API access built around that model. Layman's Analogy An AI model is like a brain that's been educated on a specific curriculum. A language model \"went to school\" reading the internet; an image model \"went to art school\" looking at millions of pictures. The model itself is just the trained brain — products and applications are built on top of it."},{"url":"topics/api.html","title":"API (Application Programming Interface)","definition":"An API is a set of rules and protocols that allows different software programs to communicate with each other — like a waiter taking orders between you (the application) and the kitchen (the service).","headings":["What is an API?","APIs in AI","REST APIs"],"text":"API (Application Programming Interface) An API is a set of rules and protocols that allows different software programs to communicate with each other — like a waiter taking orders between you (the application) and the kitchen (the service). What is an API? An API defines how one piece of software can request services from another. When you use a weather app, it calls a weather API to get the forecast data. When a developer uses Claude in their application, they call Anthropic's API to send prompts and receive responses. APIs are the glue of modern software. They allow different systems — built by different companies, in different languages, running on different servers — to work together seamlessly. APIs in AI AI companies provide APIs so developers can integrate AI models into their own applications. Instead of building and training your own model (which costs millions), you call an API and pay per use. Anthropic's Claude API, OpenAI's API, and Google's Gemini API are the main ones. The Model Context Protocol is itself an API standard — it defines how AI agents communicate with external tools and data sources. REST APIs Most web APIs use REST (Representational State Transfer) — a pattern where you send HTTP requests (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to specific URLs and receive JSON data back. For example, sending a POST request to api.anthropic.com/v1/messages with a prompt returns Claude's response. Layman's Analogy An API is like a restaurant menu. The menu (API documentation) tells you what you can order (available operations). You tell the waiter (API request) what you want. The kitchen (server) prepares it, and the waiter brings back your food (API response). You don't need to know how the kitchen works — just how to read the menu."},{"url":"topics/applications.html","title":"Applications (Software by Platform)","definition":"An application (app) is a software program built to perform tasks for users. Applications are categorized by where they run: in a web browser, on a phone, or on a desktop computer. Each platform has different technologies, trade-offs, and deployment methods.","headings":["The Application Hierarchy","Quick Comparison","Which Platform Should You Choose?"],"text":"Applications (Software by Platform) An application (app) is a software program built to perform tasks for users. Applications are categorized by where they run: in a web browser, on a phone, or on a desktop computer. Each platform has different technologies, trade-offs, and deployment methods. The Application Hierarchy Click any box below to learn more about that category: Applications &#9474; &#9500;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9532;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9472;&#9508; Web Apps Run in a browser Mobile Apps Run on a phone Desktop Apps Run on a computer Static Frontend only Full-Stack Frontend + backend Native iOS or Android Cross-Platform One codebase Native Per OS Cross-Platform Electron, Tauri Quick Comparison Platform Where it runs Languages Distribution Web App Browser (Chrome, Safari, etc.) HTML, CSS, JavaScript URL — instant access Mobile App Phone (iOS/Android) Swift, Kotlin, Dart, JS App Store / Play Store Desktop App Computer (Windows/Mac/Linux) C++, Rust, C#, JS Download / installer AI has dramatically lowered the barrier to building applications across all platforms. Static websites and simple web apps are the easiest for AI to generate — a single prompt can produce a complete, working site. Mobile and desktop apps require more human guidance but AI handles boilerplate, UI layouts, and repetitive logic extremely well. Which Platform Should You Choose? Start with the web — lowest friction, no app store approval, works everywhere Go mobile when you need push notifications, camera/GPS access, or offline capability Go desktop when you need heavy computation, file system access, or OS-level integration Many modern applications exist on multiple platforms. For example, Spotify has a web app, mobile app, and desktop app — all sharing some code but adapted to each platform's strengths. Original Hierarchy Diagram"},{"url":"topics/artificial-intelligence.html","title":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","definition":"Artificial Intelligence is the field of computer science focused on creating systems that can perform tasks that normally require human intelligence — such as understanding language, recognizing patterns, making decisions, and learning from experience.","headings":["What is AI?","Types of AI","Key Subfields"],"text":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) Artificial Intelligence is the field of computer science focused on creating systems that can perform tasks that normally require human intelligence — such as understanding language, recognizing patterns, making decisions, and learning from experience. What is AI? AI is a broad term covering any computer system designed to mimic human cognitive abilities. This ranges from simple rule-based systems (like spam filters) to complex neural networks that can write essays, generate images, or drive cars. Modern AI is dominated by machine learning — systems that learn from data rather than following hand-written rules. The most powerful current AI systems are large language models like Claude , which can understand and generate human language. Types of AI Narrow AI (ANI): AI designed for one specific task — like playing chess, translating languages, or recommending movies. All current AI is narrow AI. General AI (AGI): A hypothetical AI that could perform any intellectual task a human can. This does not yet exist. Superintelligent AI (ASI): A hypothetical AI that surpasses human intelligence in every domain. This is a subject of safety research at companies like Anthropic. Key Subfields Machine Learning — Learning from data Deep Learning — Neural networks with many layers Natural Language Processing — Understanding human language Computer Vision — Understanding images and video Robotics — AI controlling physical machines Layman's Analogy AI is like teaching a computer to think. Just as a child learns to recognize cats by seeing thousands of pictures of cats, an AI system learns to perform tasks by processing large amounts of examples. The difference is that computers can process millions of examples in hours, while humans take years."},{"url":"topics/canonical-urls.html","title":"Canonical URLs","definition":"A canonical URL is a tag that tells search engines \"this is the official version of this page\" — preventing duplicate content problems when the same content is accessible at multiple URLs.","headings":["The Problem","The Solution","2026 Context","Rules"],"text":"Canonical URLs A canonical URL is a tag that tells search engines \"this is the official version of this page\" — preventing duplicate content problems when the same content is accessible at multiple URLs. The Problem The same page can often be reached via multiple URLs: https://yoursite.com/article https://yoursite.com/article/ (trailing slash) https://yoursite.com/article?ref=twitter (with tracking parameters) http://yoursite.com/article (HTTP vs HTTPS) https://www.yoursite.com/article (www vs non-www) Google sees these as five different pages with identical content. This splits your SEO value across all of them instead of concentrating it on one. Canonical tags solve this. The Solution <link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://yoursite.com/article\"> Add this to the <head> of every page. It tells search engines: \"Regardless of how you arrived at this page, the official URL is this one. Give all the SEO credit here.\" 2026 Context Canonical URLs are increasingly important because AI search engines (Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude ) also rely on canonical signals to determine which version of a page to ingest and cite. Without clear canonicalization, AI systems might cite a URL with tracking parameters — or worse, ignore your page entirely because it looks like a duplicate. Rules Every page should have a canonical tag, even if it only has one URL (self-referencing canonical) Always use the HTTPS version Pick one style (www or non-www) and stick with it Pick one style (trailing slash or not) and stick with it The canonical URL should be the fully qualified URL (including https:// ) Layman's Analogy Imagine a book published under three different covers with slightly different titles, but the same content inside. Libraries would catalog it as three separate books, splitting reviews and lending records. A canonical URL is like telling libraries: \"These are all the same book — please file everything under this one ISBN.\" All the \"credit\" consolidates under one entry."},{"url":"topics/checkpoints.html","title":"Checkpoints & Rewind","definition":"A checkpoint is an automatic snapshot of a Claude Code session — its messages, file changes, and context — that you can rewind to, letting you undo work or explore a different approach safely.","headings":["What are Checkpoints?","Rewinding","Checkpoints vs. Git"],"text":"Checkpoints & Rewind A checkpoint is an automatic snapshot of a Claude Code session — its messages, file changes, and context — that you can rewind to, letting you undo work or explore a different approach safely. What are Checkpoints? Every time you submit a prompt, Claude Code quietly saves a checkpoint. If a change goes wrong or you simply want to try something else, you can return to any earlier checkpoint instead of manually unpicking the work. Checkpoints persist across sessions and are kept for 30 days by default. Rewinding Press Esc twice, or use the /rewind slash command , to open the checkpoint browser. Picking a checkpoint offers five choices: Option Effect Restore code and conversation Revert both files and messages to that point Restore conversation Rewind messages only; keep current code Restore code Revert file changes only; keep the conversation Summarize from here Compress the conversation forward to free context space Never mind Cancel and stay where you are Checkpoints vs. Git Checkpoints are not a replacement for version control . They are instant and capture the conversation as well as files, but they are session-based and don't track changes made outside Claude Code (or by shell commands like rm and mv ). The healthy pattern: use checkpoints for rapid experimentation, and commit finished work to git for a permanent, auditable history. Layman's Analogy Checkpoints are like the quick-save feature in a video game. Before a tricky section you save, attempt it, and if it goes badly you reload and try a different tactic — no progress permanently lost. Git, by contrast, is like writing your achievements into the official record book: slower, deliberate, and permanent."},{"url":"topics/claude-code.html","title":"Claude Code","definition":"Claude Code is an agentic coding tool by Anthropic that lives in your terminal, understands your codebase, and helps you code faster through natural language commands.","headings":["What is Claude Code?","How It Works","Key Features","Configuration Files: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md & SKILL.md"],"text":"Claude Code Claude Code is an agentic coding tool by Anthropic that lives in your terminal, understands your codebase, and helps you code faster through natural language commands. What is Claude Code? Claude Code is a command-line interface (CLI) tool that brings Claude's AI capabilities directly into your development environment. Rather than switching between a chat window and your code editor, Claude Code works where developers already work — in the terminal and inside IDEs like VS Code and JetBrains. It can read and edit files across your entire codebase, run commands, manage git workflows, execute tests, create pull requests, and build new features — all from natural language instructions. How It Works You install Claude Code via npm ( npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code ), then run claude in any project directory. It indexes your codebase and lets you give it tasks like \"fix the login bug\" or \"add dark mode to the settings page.\" Claude Code reads relevant files, makes changes, and can run your test suite to verify its work. Developers control how much autonomy Claude Code has — from approving every file change to letting it work independently on routine tasks. Key Features Multi-file editing and refactoring Git operations (commits, PRs, branch management) Running tests and fixing failures Extending with skills and MCP servers Background agents for parallel tasks (Agent View, May 2026) Configuration Files: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md & SKILL.md Claude Code is steered by three kinds of Markdown files. They are easy to confuse because they all hold plain-English instructions — but they differ in when they load and which tools read them . File What it is When it loads CLAUDE.md Claude Code's project memory — coding conventions, build commands, architecture notes. The \"how to work here\" file. Always — read into context at the start of every session. AGENTS.md A vendor-neutral, open standard for the same kind of project instructions — read by 60+ AI coding tools, not just Claude. Natively in tools that adopt the standard (Cursor, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot, Windsurf…). Claude Code reads it only when imported via @AGENTS.md . SKILL.md The defining file of a single skill — a reusable capability for one specific task, with YAML frontmatter plus instructions. On demand — only its name and description load at startup; the full file loads when the skill is triggered. The core distinction is always-on context vs. on-demand capability . CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md are standing instructions — rules that apply to everything Claude does in the project, so they sit in context the whole session. SKILL.md is a capability — a procedure for one kind of task, kept out of context until it is actually needed (a token-saving design called progressive disclosure ). CLAUDE.md vs. AGENTS.md is a question of ownership , not behavior. CLAUDE.md is Claude-specific. AGENTS.md is an open standard — now stewarded by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation — that more than 60 tools (Cursor, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot, Windsurf, Gemini CLI, Zed and others) read natively as their default instructions file . Open a project in Cursor, or run Codex against a repo, and an AGENTS.md in the root is picked up automatically with no setup. Claude Code is the outlier: its native file is CLAUDE.md , and it honors AGENTS.md only when you import it with @AGENTS.md . That is why teams using several AI tools keep the shared rules in AGENTS.md and import that one file into CLAUDE.md — one source of truth, every tool. Rule of thumb — if the guidance should apply to every task, it belongs in CLAUDE.md (or AGENTS.md ). If it is a step-by-step procedure for one task you repeat occasionally, make it a skill with a SKILL.md . Layman's Analogy If Claude is like a knowledgeable assistant you chat with, Claude Code is like having that assistant sitting next to you at your desk, able to directly type on your keyboard, open your files, and do the actual work — not just tell you what to do, but do it for you while you supervise. Real-World Example Stripe deployed Claude Code to 1,370 engineers. One team completed a 10,000-line Scala-to-Java migration in four days — work that was estimated to take ten engineer-weeks manually."},{"url":"topics/claude-design.html","title":"Claude Design","definition":"Claude Design is a collaborative visual creation tool by Anthropic Labs that lets users generate designs, prototypes, slides, and marketing materials through natural-language prompts .","headings":["What is Claude Design?","Key Features","Who is it for?"],"text":"Claude Design Claude Design is a collaborative visual creation tool by Anthropic Labs that lets users generate designs, prototypes, slides, and marketing materials through natural-language prompts . What is Claude Design? Launched on April 17, 2026, Claude Design is an AI-powered design tool that turns text descriptions into visual assets. You describe what you want — a landing page mockup, a presentation slide, a one-pager, a marketing poster — and Claude Design creates it. You can then iterate by giving feedback in natural language or editing elements directly. Key Features Prompt-to-design: Describe what you want, get a visual output Inline editing: Comment on specific elements, edit text directly, tweak spacing/color/layout Design system support: Apply your company's brand guidelines automatically Export options: PDF, PPTX, Canva, standalone HTML Handoff to code: Package designs into a bundle that Claude Code can implement Who is it for? Claude Design targets non-designers who need visual output — marketers creating campaign materials, product managers making mockups, founders building pitch decks, or teams creating internal presentations. It doesn't replace professional design tools like Figma for complex work, but handles the \"80% of design tasks that don't need a designer.\" Layman's Analogy Imagine describing a poster to an artist over the phone: \"Make it blue, put the logo top-left, big headline saying 'Summer Sale', product photo in the center.\" Claude Design is that artist — except instant, tireless, and able to produce dozens of variations in seconds."},{"url":"topics/claude-md.html","title":"CLAUDE.md & Memory","definition":"CLAUDE.md is Claude Code's memory file — a Markdown document of project standards, conventions, and preferences that is loaded automatically at the start of every session.","headings":["Why Memory Matters","The Memory Hierarchy","Creating and Editing Memory","Auto Memory"],"text":"CLAUDE.md & Memory CLAUDE.md is Claude Code's memory file — a Markdown document of project standards, conventions, and preferences that is loaded automatically at the start of every session. Why Memory Matters An LLM forgets everything between conversations — each session starts with an empty context window . Memory files solve this. By writing your build commands, coding conventions, and architecture notes into a CLAUDE.md , you give Claude Code persistent context it reads every time, so you never have to re-explain how your project works. The Memory Hierarchy Memory exists at several scopes, loaded together with higher levels taking precedence: Scope Location Best for Managed policy System directory Organization-wide rules Project memory ./CLAUDE.md Team standards (committed to git) User memory ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md Personal preferences (all projects) Local memory ./CLAUDE.local.md Personal project notes (git-ignored) Auto memory ~/.claude/projects/&hellip;/memory/ Notes Claude writes itself Creating and Editing Memory The /init slash command generates a starter CLAUDE.md by analyzing your codebase. The /memory command opens memory files in your editor. You can also just ask Claude conversationally — \"remember that we always use TypeScript strict mode\" — and it will offer to save the rule to the file you choose. Claude saving a new rule to a personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md after confirming the location with the user. Auto Memory Beyond the files you write, Claude Code keeps an auto memory directory where it records patterns and learnings on its own as it works — an entrypoint MEMORY.md plus optional topic files. Unlike CLAUDE.md , which you maintain by hand, auto memory is written by Claude itself and reloaded in future sessions. CLAUDE.md holds always-on standing instructions for the whole project. A skill's SKILL.md is an on-demand procedure for one task. If guidance applies to every task, it belongs in CLAUDE.md ; if it is a recipe for one occasional job, make it a skill. Layman's Analogy A CLAUDE.md is like the employee handbook you hand a new contractor on day one. It explains \"how we do things here\" — the dress code, the tools, who to ask — so they don't have to relearn it each morning. Without it, you would brief the same contractor from scratch every single day."},{"url":"topics/claude-skills.html","title":"Claude Skills","definition":"Claude Skills are reusable instruction sets that extend Claude Code's capabilities — small markdown files that tell Claude how to perform specific tasks, triggered automatically by context or manually via slash commands.","headings":["What are Claude Skills?","Skills and Slash Commands","SKILL.md vs. CLAUDE.md","How to Create a Skill"],"text":"Claude Skills Claude Skills are reusable instruction sets that extend Claude Code's capabilities — small markdown files that tell Claude how to perform specific tasks, triggered automatically by context or manually via slash commands. What are Claude Skills? A skill is a small folder containing a SKILL.md file with YAML frontmatter (name, description) and a markdown body with instructions that Claude follows when the skill is triggered. Skills let you teach Claude Code new behaviors without writing traditional code. Skills can be auto-invoked (Claude detects when they're relevant and uses them automatically) or user-invoked (triggered by typing a slash command like /commit or /security-review ). Skills and Slash Commands A slash command is anything you invoke by typing / — and a skill is one kind of slash command. The built-in commands ( /clear , /help , /model ) are fixed operations that ship with Claude Code. Skills are the customizable kind: prompt-based instructions you write yourself, invoked with /your-skill-name . Custom slash commands have in fact been merged into skills — creating a new /command now means writing a SKILL.md . And unlike a fixed built-in, a skill can also be auto-invoked by Claude when it detects the skill is relevant. SKILL.md vs. CLAUDE.md A SKILL.md is easy to confuse with a CLAUDE.md file, since both are Markdown instructions. The difference is timing : CLAUDE.md is always loaded — standing rules for the whole project — while a skill's SKILL.md loads only when triggered . Put always-relevant guidance in CLAUDE.md ; put a procedure for one occasional task in a skill. See Claude Code for the full comparison, including the vendor-neutral AGENTS.md standard. How to Create a Skill # .claude/skills/my-skill/SKILL.md --- name: security-review description: Reviews code for security vulnerabilities --- Review the code for OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities. Check for SQL injection, XSS, and auth issues. Report findings with severity levels. Layman's Analogy Think of skills like recipe cards for a chef. The chef (Claude) already knows how to cook, but the recipe card gives specific instructions for a particular dish. You can write new recipe cards anytime, and the chef will follow them. Some recipes the chef pulls out automatically when they see the ingredients; others you have to ask for by name."},{"url":"topics/claude.html","title":"Claude","definition":"Claude is a family of large language models developed by Anthropic, available as an AI assistant capable of conversation, writing, coding, analysis, and research.","headings":["What is Claude?","Key Capabilities","How to Access Claude"],"text":"Claude Claude is a family of large language models developed by Anthropic, available as an AI assistant capable of conversation, writing, coding, analysis, and research. What is Claude? Claude is an AI assistant built by Anthropic, a safety-focused AI company. It can understand and generate text, write code, analyze documents, and carry out complex tasks through natural language conversation. Claude was first released in March 2023 and has evolved rapidly since then. Claude models come in three sizes: Haiku (smallest, fastest, cheapest), Sonnet (balanced), and Opus (largest, most capable). This tiered approach lets users choose the right balance of speed, cost, and capability for their task. Key Capabilities Claude can write essays, summarize documents, translate languages, write and debug code, analyze data, create content, answer questions, and reason through complex problems. It can also use tools — browsing the web, running code, reading files, and interacting with external services through the Model Context Protocol . In 2026, Claude gained agentic capabilities — the ability to autonomously carry out multi-step tasks on a user's computer, such as managing files, using applications, and completing workflows across multiple tools. How to Access Claude Claude is available through claude.ai (web and mobile), the Claude Code CLI, API access for developers, and integrations in IDEs like VS Code and JetBrains. Layman's Analogy Think of Claude like a very knowledgeable assistant who has read millions of books and documents. You can ask it questions, give it writing tasks, or have it help you solve problems — all through regular conversation. Unlike a search engine that gives you links, Claude gives you direct answers and can do work for you."},{"url":"topics/cli.html","title":"Claude Code CLI","definition":"The CLI (Command-Line Interface) is how you launch and control Claude Code from your terminal — the claude command, its flags, and its modes for interactive use and scripted automation.","headings":["Interactive Mode vs. Print Mode","Headless Mode for Automation","Permission Modes"],"text":"Claude Code CLI The CLI (Command-Line Interface) is how you launch and control Claude Code from your terminal — the claude command, its flags, and its modes for interactive use and scripted automation. Interactive Mode vs. Print Mode Running claude with no arguments starts an interactive REPL — a back-and-forth session with conversation history, tab completion, and slash commands . Adding the -p (print) flag switches to headless mode : Claude answers a single query and exits, with no interface. Print mode is what makes Claude scriptable. Command What it does claude Start an interactive session claude -p \"query\" Answer one query, then exit (headless) claude -c Continue the most recent conversation cat log.txt | claude -p \"explain\" Pipe content in for analysis Headless Mode for Automation Because print mode reads from standard input and can emit JSON ( --output-format json ), Claude Code drops into pipelines and CI/CD jobs like any other command-line tool. A GitHub Actions workflow can run claude -p to review every pull request automatically — the same way it might run a linter or test suite. Permission Modes The --permission-mode flag controls how much autonomy Claude Code has, balancing speed against oversight: Mode Behavior default Ask for approval on risky actions plan Read-only — analyze and plan, change nothing acceptEdits Auto-accept file edits, ask for the rest auto Approve safe actions, prompt on risky ones Flags like --model (choose Opus, Sonnet, or Haiku), --allowedTools , and --max-turns give finer control still — useful when you want a fast, cheap, tightly-scoped run. Layman's Analogy Interactive mode is like having a conversation with a colleague at your desk. Print mode is like leaving them a sticky note with one task — you come back later and the answer is done. The same person, two ways of working: one for thinking together, one for handing off a job to run on its own."},{"url":"topics/context-window.html","title":"Context Window","definition":"The context window is the maximum amount of text (measured in tokens ) that an LLM can \"see\" and consider at one time — including both the input and the output.","headings":["What is a Context Window?","Why It Matters","Managing Context"],"text":"Context Window The context window is the maximum amount of text (measured in tokens ) that an LLM can \"see\" and consider at one time — including both the input and the output. What is a Context Window? Think of the context window as the AI's working memory. Everything the model needs to consider — your conversation history, system instructions, documents you've shared, and its own response — must fit within this window. Anything outside the window is invisible to the model. Claude's context window is up to 1 million tokens (roughly 750,000 words or several full-length novels). Earlier models had much smaller windows — GPT-3 had only 4,096 tokens (about 3,000 words). Why It Matters Conversation length: Longer windows mean the AI remembers more of your conversation Document analysis: Larger windows let you feed entire codebases or books to the model Complex tasks: Multi-step reasoning needs more context to track all the pieces Cost: More tokens in the context = higher API cost Managing Context When conversations exceed the context window, systems must decide what to keep and what to drop. Techniques include summarizing older messages, using RAG to fetch only relevant information, and prompt compression. Layman's Analogy The context window is like the size of your desk. A small desk (4K tokens) means you can only have one document open at a time. A huge desk (1M tokens) means you can spread out multiple books, notebooks, and reference materials simultaneously. The bigger the desk, the more information the AI can consider at once."},{"url":"topics/deep-learning.html","title":"Deep Learning","definition":"Deep Learning is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers to learn complex patterns from large amounts of data.","headings":["What is Deep Learning?","Why Did Deep Learning Take Off?"],"text":"Deep Learning Deep Learning is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers to learn complex patterns from large amounts of data. What is Deep Learning? \"Deep\" refers to the number of layers in the neural network. While a simple neural network might have 2-3 layers, deep learning models have dozens or even hundreds of layers. Each layer learns increasingly abstract features — early layers might detect edges in an image, while deeper layers recognize faces. Deep learning is the technology behind modern AI breakthroughs: image recognition, speech-to-text, language translation, and large language models . The Transformer architecture that powers models like Claude is a deep learning architecture. Why Did Deep Learning Take Off? Three factors converged around 2012: (1) massive datasets became available via the internet, (2) GPUs provided the computing power needed to train large networks, and (3) algorithmic improvements made training deeper networks practical. This combination unlocked capabilities that previous approaches couldn't achieve. Layman's Analogy Imagine a factory assembly line where each station refines a product. Raw metal enters, and each station adds more detail — cutting, shaping, polishing, assembling. Deep learning works similarly: raw data enters the first layer, and each subsequent layer extracts more refined, abstract understanding until the final layer produces the answer."},{"url":"topics/desktop-apps.html","title":"Desktop Apps","definition":"A desktop application is software installed and run directly on a computer (Windows, macOS, or Linux). Desktop apps have full access to the file system, hardware, and OS features — making them ideal for heavy computation, professional tools, and offline use.","headings":["When Do You Need a Desktop App?"],"text":"Desktop Apps A desktop application is software installed and run directly on a computer (Windows, macOS, or Linux). Desktop apps have full access to the file system, hardware, and OS features — making them ideal for heavy computation, professional tools, and offline use. Native Desktop Apps Built specifically for one operating system using platform-native tools. Offers maximum performance and the most natural user experience for that OS. Platform Languages / Frameworks Examples Windows C#/.NET (WinUI), C++ (Win32) File Explorer, Visual Studio macOS Swift/SwiftUI, Objective-C Finder, Xcode, Final Cut Pro Linux C/C++ (GTK, Qt), Rust GIMP, Blender Pros: Best performance, smallest memory footprint, OS-native look and feel, full hardware access. Cons: Separate codebase per OS, smaller developer pools for some platforms. Cross-Platform Desktop Apps One codebase that runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux. The two dominant approaches in 2026: Electron Packages a full Chromium browser + Node.js runtime with your web app. Write HTML/CSS/JavaScript, get a desktop app. Used by: VS Code, Discord, Slack, Notion, Figma Pros: Massive ecosystem, easy for web developers, mature and stable Cons: Large binary size (~60MB+), high memory usage (~200MB+), bundles entire Chromium Tauri Uses the OS's native webview (not bundled Chromium) with a Rust backend. The modern alternative to Electron, growing rapidly since 2024. Used by: Growing number of developer tools, AI applications, note-taking apps Pros: Tiny binaries (~2MB), low memory (~50MB), fast startup, better security model Cons: Smaller ecosystem, requires Rust knowledge for backend, inconsistent webview rendering across OS New in 2026: Tauri 2.x supports mobile targets too (iOS + Android from same codebase) Comparison Electron Tauri Binary size ~60MB+ ~2MB RAM usage ~200MB ~50MB Startup time Slower Under 1 second Backend language JavaScript (Node.js) Rust Frontend Any web framework Any web framework Ecosystem maturity Very mature Growing fast (55% YoY) Cross-platform desktop apps built with Electron or Tauri use web technologies (HTML/CSS/JS) for the frontend — meaning AI generates the UI layer just as easily as for web apps. The challenge is the native backend: Electron's Node.js layer is straightforward for AI, while Tauri's Rust backend is harder for AI to generate correctly due to Rust's strict type system and ownership rules. For desktop AI tools specifically, Tauri + Rust is increasingly popular due to performance advantages with large model inference. When Do You Need a Desktop App? Heavy computation — Video editing, 3D rendering, large dataset processing File system access — Managing local files, bulk operations Offline-first — Must work without internet OS integration — System tray, keyboard shortcuts, notifications Performance-critical — Real-time audio/video, gaming If none of these apply, a web app is usually simpler to build and distribute. Layman's Analogy A native desktop app is like a custom-built workshop tool — perfectly fitted for one workbench, extremely efficient. A cross-platform app (Electron/Tauri) is like a multi-tool — works in any workshop, does most jobs well, trades some specialization for versatility. Electron is the heavier multi-tool with every attachment; Tauri is the sleek one that weighs almost nothing."},{"url":"topics/embedding.html","title":"Embedding","definition":"An embedding is a numerical representation of text (or images, audio, etc.) as a list of numbers — a way to convert meaning into math so that AI systems can measure similarity between concepts.","headings":["What is an Embedding?","How Embeddings Are Used"],"text":"Embedding An embedding is a numerical representation of text (or images, audio, etc.) as a list of numbers — a way to convert meaning into math so that AI systems can measure similarity between concepts. What is an Embedding? Computers can't understand words directly — they work with numbers. An embedding converts a word, sentence, or document into a vector (a list of hundreds or thousands of numbers) that captures its meaning. Words with similar meanings end up as similar vectors. For example, the embeddings for \"king\" and \"queen\" would be close together in vector space, while \"king\" and \"banana\" would be far apart. This allows AI systems to understand that \"happy\" and \"joyful\" mean similar things, even though they're different words. How Embeddings Are Used RAG : Finding relevant documents by comparing query embeddings to document embeddings Search: Semantic search that understands meaning, not just keywords Recommendations: Finding similar items (movies, products, articles) Clustering: Grouping similar documents together Inside Transformers : The first layer of any LLM converts tokens into embeddings Layman's Analogy Imagine a map where cities are placed not by geography but by culture. Paris and Rome would be close (both European, romantic, historic). Tokyo and Seoul would be close. New York and London would be close. An embedding is like giving every concept a GPS coordinate on a \"meaning map\" — similar meanings have nearby coordinates."},{"url":"topics/fine-tuning.html","title":"Fine-tuning","definition":"Fine-tuning is the process of taking a pre-trained AI model and further training it on a smaller, specialized dataset to improve its performance on specific tasks or domains.","headings":["What is Fine-tuning?","Fine-tuning vs. RAG vs. Prompting","When to Fine-tune"],"text":"Fine-tuning Fine-tuning is the process of taking a pre-trained AI model and further training it on a smaller, specialized dataset to improve its performance on specific tasks or domains. What is Fine-tuning? Training an LLM from scratch costs millions of dollars and requires enormous datasets. Fine-tuning is the shortcut: you take an already-trained model (which already knows language) and give it additional training on your specific data — medical records, legal documents, customer support conversations, or your company's coding style. After fine-tuning, the model retains its general capabilities but becomes significantly better at your specific use case. Fine-tuning vs. RAG vs. Prompting Prompting : Cheapest, fastest. Just tell the model what to do. Works for most cases. RAG : Give the model access to your documents at query time. Good for knowledge-heavy tasks. Fine-tuning: Most expensive, changes the model itself. Use when you need the model to adopt a specific style, format, or behavior that can't be achieved through prompting. When to Fine-tune Fine-tuning makes sense when: (1) you have a consistent format or style the model must follow, (2) you need faster responses without long prompts, or (3) prompting alone can't achieve the required quality. It's usually the last resort after trying prompting and RAG. Layman's Analogy Fine-tuning is like taking a general-practice doctor and sending them to specialize in cardiology. They already know medicine (pre-training), but the additional training (fine-tuning) makes them excellent at one specific area. They keep their general knowledge but gain deep expertise in hearts."},{"url":"topics/formats.html","title":"Formats","definition":"Formats are the structured ways you package text and data when communicating with AI models . Choosing the right format directly affects how well the AI understands your input and how usable its output is.","headings":["Why Formats Matter for AI","The Four Key Formats","Practical Decision Guide","Combining Formats"],"text":"Formats Formats are the structured ways you package text and data when communicating with AI models . Choosing the right format directly affects how well the AI understands your input and how usable its output is. Why Formats Matter for AI LLMs process everything as text. But not all text is equal — structured text gets better results than unstructured walls of words. The format you choose determines: How clearly the model understands your intent How reliably it follows complex, multi-section instructions Whether its output is usable by other systems (code, databases, automation tools ) Whether humans can also read and maintain the content The Four Key Formats Markdown Plain-text formatting with simple symbols ( # , ** , - ). The default for anything humans will also read: documentation, agent instructions (CLAUDE.md), RAG context, README files. Works well because LLMs saw massive amounts of Markdown in training data . Use for: Documents, instructions, context that humans also read. XML Tags Paired tags like <instructions>...</instructions> that mark clear boundaries. The strongest structural signal for Claude specifically — Anthropic has tuned Claude to pay special attention to XML structure in prompts . Use for: Production prompts, multi-section inputs, when the model must extract a specific section. JSON Key-value data format ( {\"name\": \"value\"} ). The universal standard for API communication and structured output. When you need AI to return data that code will process, JSON is the answer. Use for: API calls, structured data output, automation configs, MCP messages. HTML The markup language of the web. AI models can both read and generate HTML. Used when the output needs to render in browsers — web pages, emails, reports, design exports . Use for: Web output, rendered content, email templates, visual artifacts. Practical Decision Guide Situation Best Format Writing a system prompt for Claude XML tags for sections + Markdown for content within Agent instructions (CLAUDE.md, skills) Markdown Feeding documents to RAG Markdown Getting data for your app to use JSON (structured output) Calling an AI API JSON AI generating a web page or email HTML Multi-section prompt where the model must isolate parts XML tags Combining Formats In practice, you often combine formats. A Claude system prompt might use XML tags to structure sections, Markdown for readable content within those sections, and instruct the model to output JSON. This is normal and encouraged: <instructions> ## Your Role You are a data extraction assistant. ## Output Format Return a JSON object with these fields: - name (string) - email (string) - intent (string: \"support\", \"sales\", or \"other\") </instructions> <email> {{user's email content here}} </email>"},{"url":"topics/full-stack-apps.html","title":"Full-Stack Applications","definition":"A full-stack application is a complete software system with three layers: a frontend (what users see), a backend (server logic), and a database (persistent storage). Together, these enable dynamic behavior — user accounts, data processing, payments, and personalized experiences.","headings":["The Three Required Layers","How the Layers Communicate","Common Full-Stack Combinations (2026)","Full-Stack vs Static — When Do You Need This?"],"text":"Full-Stack Applications A full-stack application is a complete software system with three layers: a frontend (what users see), a backend (server logic), and a database (persistent storage). Together, these enable dynamic behavior — user accounts, data processing, payments, and personalized experiences. The Three Required Layers 1. Frontend (Client-Side) The user interface — what people see and interact with in their browser. Built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, often using a framework. Must have: HTML, CSS, JavaScript Common frameworks: React, Vue.js, Next.js, Svelte Responsibility: Display data, handle user interactions, send requests to the backend 2. Backend (Server-Side) The logic layer — processes requests, enforces business rules, communicates with the database, handles authentication, and sends responses back to the frontend. Must have: A server that listens for requests and sends responses Common technologies: Node.js (Express), Python (Django/FastAPI), Go, Ruby (Rails) Responsibility: API endpoints, authentication, authorization, data validation 3. Database (Data Layer) Persistent storage — where user data, content, transactions, and application state live. Without a database, the app forgets everything when the server restarts. Must have: A way to store and query data Relational (SQL): PostgreSQL, MySQL — structured tables with relationships Document (NoSQL): MongoDB, Redis — flexible JSON-like documents Responsibility: Persist data, handle queries, maintain data integrity How the Layers Communicate User clicks \"Sign Up\" → Frontend sends POST /api/users with form data → Backend validates data, hashes password → Database stores new user record ← Backend responds: { success: true } ← Frontend shows \"Welcome!\" message Common Full-Stack Combinations (2026) Stack Name Frontend Backend Database MERN React Express (Node.js) MongoDB Next.js + Supabase React (Next.js) Next.js API routes PostgreSQL (Supabase) Django Templates or React Django (Python) PostgreSQL Rails Hotwire or React Ruby on Rails PostgreSQL AI can scaffold a full-stack app — generating API routes, database schemas, and UI components — but you still need human judgment for architecture decisions, security (authentication, authorization, input validation), and deployment configuration. AI accelerates building the layers; humans ensure they work together securely. Tools like Cursor, Claude Code, and Bolt.new can generate a working full-stack prototype from a description, but production apps need careful review. Full-Stack vs Static — When Do You Need This? You need a full-stack app when your project requires any of these: User accounts (sign up, log in, profiles) Saving user data (posts, orders, settings) Payments (Stripe, PayPal integration) Real-time features (chat, notifications, live updates) Admin dashboards (managing content or users) If you don't need any of these, a static website is simpler, cheaper, and faster. Layman's Analogy A full-stack app is like a bank. The lobby and forms (frontend) are what you see. The tellers and vault policies (backend) decide what's allowed. The vault itself (database) holds your money. All three must work together — a lobby without a vault is just a pretty room, and a vault without a lobby is inaccessible."},{"url":"topics/github-pages.html","title":"GitHub Pages (Deploying a Static Site)","definition":"GitHub Pages is a free hosting service by GitHub that turns a repository of HTML files into a live website with a real URL — the standard way to host static sites like documentation, portfolios, and glossaries.","headings":["What is GitHub Pages?","Prerequisites","Step-by-Step: Deploy Your Site","Updating Your Site","Optional: Custom Domain","Your Live URL","GitHub Pages vs Netlify","Why GitHub Pages?"],"text":"GitHub Pages (Deploying a Static Site) GitHub Pages is a free hosting service by GitHub that turns a repository of HTML files into a live website with a real URL — the standard way to host static sites like documentation, portfolios, and glossaries. What is GitHub Pages? GitHub is where developers store and collaborate on code. GitHub Pages is a feature that takes a repository (a project folder on GitHub) and serves its HTML files as a public website. No server setup, no hosting fees, no configuration. You push your files, GitHub hosts them. Your site gets a URL like https://yourusername.github.io/your-project/ . You can also connect a custom domain (like yourbrand.com ) later. Prerequisites You need two tools installed on your computer: Git — Version control (tracks changes to your files). Check: git --version GitHub CLI (gh) — GitHub's command-line tool. Check: gh --version If you don't have them: # Install Git (if not already installed) # Download from https://git-scm.com/downloads # Install GitHub CLI winget install GitHub.cli # Windows brew install gh # macOS # Authenticate (one-time setup) gh auth login Step-by-Step: Deploy Your Site Step 1: Initialize Git Open your terminal, navigate to your project folder, and initialize a Git repository: cd /path/to/your-project git init What this does: Creates a hidden .git folder that tracks all changes to your files. Your project is now a Git repository. Step 2: Stage and Commit Your Files git add . git commit -m \"Initial commit: project files\" What this does: git add . tells Git to track all files in the folder. git commit saves a snapshot of your project with a description message. Think of it as pressing \"Save\" with a label. Step 3: Create the GitHub Repository and Push gh repo create your-project-name --public --source=. --push What this does: This single command does three things at once: Creates a new repository on your GitHub account Connects your local folder to that repository Pushes (uploads) all your files to GitHub Use --public for a site anyone can view, or --private if you want to restrict access (GitHub Pages still works with private repos on paid plans). Step 4: Enable GitHub Pages gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pages -X POST \\ -f source.branch=main \\ -f source.path=/ Replace {owner} with your GitHub username and {repo} with the repository name. What this does: Tells GitHub to serve your main branch as a website, using the root folder ( / ) as the source. Alternative: You can also do this in the browser — go to your repository on github.com → Settings → Pages → select \"main\" branch → Save. Step 5: Visit Your Live Site Wait about 60 seconds, then visit: https://yourusername.github.io/your-project-name/ Your site is now live on the internet with HTTPS, accessible to anyone. Updating Your Site After making changes to your files locally, push updates with: git add . git commit -m \"Description of what you changed\" git push GitHub Pages automatically rebuilds your site within seconds of each push. No redeployment needed. Optional: Custom Domain To use your own domain (e.g., glossary.yourbrand.com ): Buy a domain from any registrar (Namecheap, Cloudflare, Google Domains) Add a CNAME record pointing to yourusername.github.io In your repo, go to Settings → Pages → Custom domain → enter your domain GitHub provides free HTTPS for custom domains automatically Your Live URL Once deployed, your site is immediately accessible at: https://yourusername.github.io/your-repo-name/ This is the fastest way to see your deployment — no dashboard needed. Just construct the URL from your GitHub username and repository name. For example, user janedoe with repo my-portfolio would visit https://janedoe.github.io/my-portfolio/ . GitHub Pages vs Netlify Both are popular free options for hosting static sites. Here's how they compare: GitHub Pages Netlify Cost Always free (public repos) Free tier, 100GB bandwidth/month limit Setup git push — that's it Connect repo or drag-and-drop What it hosts Static files only (HTML/CSS/JS) Static files + serverless functions Build tools Jekyll only (or pre-built files) Many (React, Vue, Hugo, Next.js, etc.) Forms Not included Built-in form handling Custom domains 1 per site Multiple per site Default URL username.github.io/repo project.netlify.app Verdict: For plain HTML/CSS/JS sites, GitHub Pages is the simpler choice — zero configuration, no bandwidth limits that matter, and you already use Git for version control. Netlify is better when you need build pipelines for frameworks, serverless functions, or built-in form handling. Neither URL is SEO-friendly by default; use a custom domain for that. What GitHub Pages cannot host: Server-side applications (Node/Express, Django, Rails), anything requiring a database, or apps needing server-side processing at request time. It serves static files only. Why GitHub Pages? Free: No hosting costs for static sites Professional: Real URL with HTTPS, not file:///C:/... Version controlled: Every change is tracked in Git history Auto-deploys: Push code → site updates automatically Reliable: Backed by GitHub's infrastructure (99.9% uptime) Industry standard: Used by millions of developers and companies Layman's Analogy Dragging an HTML file into your browser is like printing a flyer and showing it to someone by hand. Deploying to GitHub Pages is like putting that flyer on a billboard on a public street — anyone with the address can see it, it's always there, and you can update it anytime by pushing a button from your desk. Complete Example: From Zero to Live # Navigate to your project cd /c/PROJECTS/kursevi-ai-glossary # Initialize Git and make first commit git init git add . git commit -m \"Initial commit: AI Glossary with 36 topics and quiz\" # Create repo on GitHub and push (one command) gh repo create kursevi-ai-glossary --public --source=. --push # Enable GitHub Pages gh api repos/YOUR_USERNAME/kursevi-ai-glossary/pages \\ -X POST -f source.branch=main -f source.path=/ # Wait ~60 seconds, then visit: # https://YOUR_USERNAME.github.io/kursevi-ai-glossary/"},{"url":"topics/google-search-console.html","title":"Google Search Console","definition":"Google Search Console (GSC) is a free tool from Google that shows you how Google sees your website — what's indexed, what searches lead to your pages, and what technical issues need fixing.","headings":["What is Google Search Console?","Setup (10 Minutes)","What You Can Do","First Things to Check"],"text":"Google Search Console Google Search Console (GSC) is a free tool from Google that shows you how Google sees your website — what's indexed, what searches lead to your pages, and what technical issues need fixing. What is Google Search Console? GSC is the closest thing to a direct line into Google's brain regarding your website. It answers questions like: Which pages has Google found? What search queries bring people to my site? Are there broken pages? Is my site fast enough? Is my sitemap working? Is my structured data valid? In 2026, mastering GSC is not optional — it's essential for anyone serious about SEO. With AI-driven search (Google AI Overviews) now shaping how content is surfaced, GSC data helps you understand both traditional and AI search visibility. Setup (10 Minutes) Go to search.google.com/search-console Click \"Add property\" → choose \"URL prefix\" (easier) or \"Domain\" (recommended for full coverage) Verify ownership (add an HTML tag to your site, upload a file, or use DNS verification) Submit your sitemap.xml Wait 24-48 hours for data to start appearing What You Can Do Performance report: See which searches bring clicks, your average position, click-through rates Index coverage: Which pages Google has indexed, which were excluded, and why Sitemap submission: Submit and monitor your sitemap.xml URL inspection: Check how Google sees any specific page on your site Core Web Vitals: Performance metrics for your pages Structured data validation: Check if your JSON-LD schema is correct Manual actions: Alerts if Google has penalized your site for any reason First Things to Check After setup, check these immediately: Are all your important pages indexed? (Pages → Indexing) Is your sitemap submitted and processed? (Sitemaps) Are there any errors? (Check for \"Not indexed\" pages and understand why) What queries are you appearing for? (Performance → Queries) Layman's Analogy Google Search Console is like the dashboard of your car. You can drive without looking at it, but you'll miss the check-engine light (indexing errors), the fuel gauge (traffic data), and the speedometer (page performance). It doesn't drive the car for you, but it tells you everything you need to know to drive well and avoid breakdowns."},{"url":"topics/hallucination.html","title":"Hallucination","definition":"A hallucination is when an AI model generates information that sounds confident and plausible but is factually incorrect, fabricated, or nonsensical.","headings":["What is an AI Hallucination?","Common Examples","Mitigation Strategies"],"text":"Hallucination A hallucination is when an AI model generates information that sounds confident and plausible but is factually incorrect, fabricated, or nonsensical. What is an AI Hallucination? LLMs don't \"know\" things the way humans do — they predict likely token sequences based on patterns in their training data . Sometimes these patterns produce text that reads well but is simply wrong: fake citations, invented facts, non-existent APIs, or historical events that never happened. Hallucinations are a fundamental challenge in AI because the model expresses fabricated information with the same confidence as accurate information — making it hard for users to tell the difference without verification. Common Examples Citing academic papers that don't exist Inventing plausible-sounding statistics Describing features of software that were never built Attributing quotes to people who never said them Mitigation Strategies RAG : Ground responses in retrieved sources Citation requirements: Ask the model to cite sources (makes fabrication more obvious) Low temperature : Reduce randomness for factual tasks Human verification: Always fact-check critical information Safety training: Models like Claude are trained to say \"I don't know\" rather than guess Layman's Analogy A hallucination is like a student who didn't study for the exam but writes confidently anyway — filling in plausible-sounding answers that happen to be completely wrong. The essay reads well, the logic seems sound, but the facts are made up. That's what AI does when it hallucinates."},{"url":"topics/hooks.html","title":"Hooks","definition":"Hooks are automated actions that Claude Code runs in response to specific events during a session — such as before a file is written or after a command completes — enabling validation, formatting, and custom automation.","headings":["What are Hooks?","Hook Events","Hook Types","What Hooks Are Good For"],"text":"Hooks Hooks are automated actions that Claude Code runs in response to specific events during a session — such as before a file is written or after a command completes — enabling validation, formatting, and custom automation. What are Hooks? A hook ties an event to an action . You configure hooks in a settings file ( settings.json ), and Claude Code fires them automatically when the matching event occurs. Because they run deterministically — every time, no AI judgment involved — hooks turn \"please remember to&hellip;\" instructions into guarantees. Hook Events Claude Code supports roughly 29 events. The most commonly used: Event Fires when&hellip; Typical use PreToolUse Before a tool runs Validate or block a risky action PostToolUse After a tool succeeds Auto-format code, scan for secrets UserPromptSubmit A prompt is submitted Validate input before processing SessionStart A session begins Set up environment variables Stop Claude finishes responding Check that all tasks are complete Hook Types A hook's action can be a shell command (the default), an HTTP webhook, an LLM-evaluated prompt , an MCP tool call, or a subagent ( agent ). Command hooks receive event data as JSON on standard input and communicate back through exit codes — exit 0 to continue, exit 2 to block the operation. What Hooks Are Good For Common patterns include auto-formatting every file Claude edits, scanning new code for hardcoded secrets, blocking dangerous shell commands like rm -rf / , running tests before a commit, and sending the team a notification when a long task finishes. Hooks are how a team encodes its non-negotiable rules into the workflow itself. Hooks execute arbitrary shell commands on your machine — use them at your own risk. Validate inputs, quote variables, skip sensitive files, and test a hook in isolation before relying on it. Layman's Analogy Hooks are like the automatic sensors in a modern car. You don't have to remember to turn on the headlights at dusk or lock the doors when you drive off — the car detects the event and does it for you, every time. Hooks do the same for your coding workflow: the trigger happens, the action follows, no one has to remember."},{"url":"topics/html-vs-full-stack.html","title":"HTML vs Full Stack App","definition":"An HTML page is a static document displayed in a browser. A full-stack application is a complete software system with a frontend (what users see), backend (server logic), and database (data storage) — capable of dynamic behavior, user accounts, and data processing.","headings":["What is an HTML Page?","What is a Full-Stack Application?","When to Use Which"],"text":"HTML vs Full Stack App An HTML page is a static document displayed in a browser. A full-stack application is a complete software system with a frontend (what users see), backend (server logic), and database (data storage) — capable of dynamic behavior, user accounts, and data processing. What is an HTML Page? HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the language that defines the structure of web pages. A plain HTML page is like a printed document — it displays information but can't remember anything, process payments, or let users log in. This AI Glossary you're reading right now is a static HTML website. Static HTML pages are simple, fast, cheap to host, and require no server. They're perfect for blogs, documentation, portfolios, and informational sites. What is a Full-Stack Application? A full-stack app has three layers: Frontend: The user interface (built with HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, etc.) Backend: Server logic that processes requests (built with Node.js, Python, Go, etc.) Database: Persistent storage for data (PostgreSQL, MongoDB, etc.) Full-stack apps can have user accounts, process data, send emails, handle payments, and provide personalized experiences. Think: Gmail, Facebook, Amazon, Spotify. When to Use Which Static HTML Full-Stack App Blog, portfolio Social media, e-commerce Documentation User dashboards Landing pages SaaS applications No server needed Requires server + database Free/cheap hosting More complex, more expensive Layman's Analogy An HTML page is like a printed brochure — it gives you information, but you can't interact with it beyond reading. A full-stack app is like a bank branch — you can check your balance, transfer money, open accounts, and the bank remembers you next time you visit. The brochure is simpler and cheaper, but the bank branch can actually do things for you."},{"url":"topics/html.html","title":"HTML (in AI Context)","definition":"HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard format for web pages. In AI, it matters in two ways: as an output format when AI generates web content, and as a source format that gets preprocessed before AI can learn from it.","headings":["HTML as AI Output","HTML as AI Input (Preprocessing)","Practical Use: When to Use HTML with AI","HTML vs. Markdown for AI"],"text":"HTML (in AI Context) HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard format for web pages. In AI, it matters in two ways: as an output format when AI generates web content, and as a source format that gets preprocessed before AI can learn from it. HTML as AI Output LLMs like Claude are excellent at generating HTML. This is the primary practical use of HTML in AI workflows: Web artifacts: Claude can generate complete, interactive web pages with HTML + CSS + JavaScript Claude Design exports: Designs export as standalone HTML files Email templates: AI generates formatted HTML emails Reports and dashboards: Generating visual, browser-renderable output Prototypes: Quickly creating functional UI prototypes from descriptions HTML as AI Input (Preprocessing) Here's a key nuance: LLMs are not trained on raw HTML in the way many people assume. Training data from web crawls (like Common Crawl) goes through heavy preprocessing — HTML tags get stripped, text gets extracted, the document is cleaned. The model rarely sees raw <h1> or <div class=\"...\"> tags during training. This is precisely why Markdown is more effective for prompting : Markdown survives preprocessing because it IS plain text with a few special characters. HTML gets stripped away. Practical Use: When to Use HTML with AI DO ask AI to generate HTML when you need web-renderable output DO use HTML when building web applications with AI assistance DON'T use HTML to structure your prompts (use XML tags or Markdown instead) DON'T feed raw HTML to AI for analysis if you can extract the text first HTML vs. Markdown for AI A common question: should I give AI content as HTML or Markdown? Markdown wins for input — it's more token -efficient (fewer characters to express the same structure), and models understand it natively from training data. HTML wins for output — when the result needs to render visually in a browser or email client. Layman's Analogy HTML is like the blueprint of a building — it tells the browser exactly where every wall, window, and door goes. When you view a website, your browser reads the HTML blueprint and constructs what you see. AI is great at drawing these blueprints (generating HTML), but when AI needs to learn from web content, it reads the extracted text, not the raw blueprint markup."},{"url":"topics/human-in-the-loop.html","title":"Human-in-the-Loop (HITL)","definition":"Human-in-the-loop is a design pattern where AI systems pause at critical decision points to request human review or approval before proceeding — balancing automation speed with human judgment for safety, compliance, and trust.","headings":["What is Human-in-the-Loop?","The Calibrated Autonomy Pattern","Where the Gate Lives","Common HITL Patterns","Why It Matters in 2026"],"text":"Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Human-in-the-loop is a design pattern where AI systems pause at critical decision points to request human review or approval before proceeding — balancing automation speed with human judgment for safety, compliance, and trust. What is Human-in-the-Loop? Not every AI decision should be fully autonomous. Some actions are irreversible, high-stakes, or legally sensitive. Human-in-the-loop places a checkpoint at these critical moments: the AI does the heavy lifting (research, drafting, analysis), and a human validates the final decision before execution. In 2026, 57% of companies have AI agents in production, and 80% have encountered risky autonomous behavior. HITL is no longer a nice-to-have — it's an operational and regulatory requirement. The Calibrated Autonomy Pattern The modern approach isn't \"always ask a human\" or \"never ask a human\" — it's calibrated autonomy: Full autonomy for low-risk, reversible actions (reading files, searching data, generating drafts) Human approval for high-risk, irreversible actions (sending emails, making payments, deleting data, deploying code) Confidence-based escalation: When the AI's confidence drops below a threshold, it escalates to a human rather than guessing Where the Gate Lives The approval gate lives outside the model. The AI doesn't decide when to ask for permission — a policy engine evaluates each proposed action against rules (cost thresholds, permission levels, risk categories). If the policy returns REQUIRE_APPROVAL, the system holds the action in a pending queue until a human approves. This separation is critical: the model proposes, the system enforces. You don't rely on the AI's judgment about its own limitations. Common HITL Patterns Approve/reject: AI proposes an action, human clicks approve or deny before execution Review-then-send: AI drafts an email or message, human reviews and edits before sending Escalation on low confidence: AI handles routine cases autonomously, flags uncertain ones for human review Periodic audit: AI acts autonomously but a human reviews a sample of decisions after the fact Shadow mode: AI proposes actions, human executes them initially — building trust before granting autonomy Why It Matters in 2026 The EU AI Act Article 14 (enforceable August 2, 2026) mandates human oversight capabilities for high-risk AI systems. Organizations deploying AI in healthcare, finance, hiring, or critical infrastructure must implement HITL mechanisms — it's now a legal requirement, not just a best practice. Layman's Analogy Think of HITL like a self-driving car that handles highway driving perfectly but hands control back to you in construction zones. The car does 95% of the driving autonomously, but for the tricky 5% where the stakes are high, it says \"your turn\" — not because it can't try, but because the consequences of a mistake are too severe. Real-World Example: Claude Code Claude Code implements calibrated autonomy: it reads files and searches code freely (low-risk, reversible), but asks permission before writing files, executing shell commands, or making network requests (higher-risk, potentially irreversible). The permission gate is in the system, not in Claude's judgment — ensuring consistent safety regardless of how the model reasons."},{"url":"topics/ide.html","title":"IDE (Integrated Development Environment)","definition":"An IDE is a software application that provides a comprehensive workspace for writing, testing, and debugging code — combining a text editor, file browser, terminal, and developer tools in one window.","headings":["What is an IDE?","Popular IDEs","IDEs and AI"],"text":"IDE (Integrated Development Environment) An IDE is a software application that provides a comprehensive workspace for writing, testing, and debugging code — combining a text editor, file browser, terminal, and developer tools in one window. What is an IDE? An IDE is where developers write code. Instead of using separate tools for editing files, running programs, and finding bugs, an IDE brings everything together. It provides features like syntax highlighting (coloring code to make it readable), auto-completion (suggesting code as you type), error detection, and integrated debugging. Popular IDEs VS Code (Visual Studio Code): The most popular IDE, free, made by Microsoft. Supports every language via extensions. JetBrains (IntelliJ, PyCharm, WebStorm): Powerful paid IDEs with deep language understanding. Xcode: Apple's IDE for building iOS/Mac apps. Android Studio: Google's IDE for Android development. IDEs and AI Modern IDEs integrate AI directly into the coding experience. Claude Code works both as a standalone terminal tool and as an extension inside VS Code and JetBrains IDEs. This means you can ask Claude to edit your code, explain functions, or run tasks without leaving your editor. AI-powered IDEs represent a shift from \"tools that help you write code\" to \"tools that write code with you\" — combining human creativity with AI's speed and knowledge. Layman's Analogy An IDE is like a fully equipped workshop for a carpenter. Instead of having your saw in one building, your workbench in another, and your measuring tools in a third, everything is in one room. The saw (compiler), workbench (editor), measuring tape (debugger), and blueprints (file browser) are all within arm's reach."},{"url":"topics/inference.html","title":"Inference","definition":"Inference is when a trained AI model generates output — the moment it actually produces a response to your prompt . It's the \"using\" phase as opposed to the \"learning\" phase.","headings":["What is Inference?","How Inference Works for LLMs","Inference Costs"],"text":"Inference Inference is when a trained AI model generates output — the moment it actually produces a response to your prompt . It's the \"using\" phase as opposed to the \"learning\" phase. What is Inference? An AI model's life has two phases: training (learning from data , which takes weeks/months and costs millions) and inference (using what it learned to generate responses, which happens in seconds). Every time you chat with Claude , you're running inference. How Inference Works for LLMs During inference, an LLM generates text one token at a time. For each token, it: Looks at all previous tokens (the context ) Calculates probabilities for every possible next token Selects one token (influenced by temperature ) Adds that token to the context and repeats This is why AI responses appear word by word — it's literally generating one token at a time. Inference Costs Running inference requires powerful hardware (GPUs/TPUs). AI APIs charge per token of inference — both input tokens (your prompt) and output tokens (the response). Faster, cheaper inference is a major area of research and competition. Layman's Analogy Training is like going to school for 16 years. Inference is like using that education at your job every day. School is expensive and takes a long time, but once you've graduated, you can quickly apply what you learned to new problems. Similarly, training a model costs millions, but each inference (each response) is cheap and fast."},{"url":"topics/json.html","title":"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)","definition":"JSON is a lightweight, human-readable data format used to structure and exchange information between systems. In AI, it's the standard way to send data to and receive structured output from AI models and APIs .","headings":["What is JSON?","Why JSON Matters for AI","Structured Outputs","JSON vs. Other Formats"],"text":"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) JSON is a lightweight, human-readable data format used to structure and exchange information between systems. In AI, it's the standard way to send data to and receive structured output from AI models and APIs . What is JSON? JSON is a way to represent data as key-value pairs in a text file. It was originally based on JavaScript syntax but is now used universally across all programming languages. When you call the Claude API , you send a JSON request and get a JSON response back. { \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-6\", \"messages\": [ { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is the capital of France?\" } ], \"max_tokens\": 100 } Why JSON Matters for AI API communication: Every AI API (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google) uses JSON for requests and responses Structured outputs: You can instruct LLMs to return JSON that follows a specific schema — making AI output directly usable by code without parsing free-form text MCP messages: The Model Context Protocol uses JSON to communicate between AI and tools Configuration: AI tools, skills , and automation workflows store settings in JSON Training data : Datasets for fine-tuning are often formatted as JSON lines (JSONL) Structured Outputs One of the most powerful patterns in 2026 AI development is \"structured outputs\" — asking a model to return JSON that conforms to a specific schema. Instead of getting free-form text you need to parse, you get predictable, typed data that your code can immediately use. // Ask Claude to extract data as structured JSON: { \"name\": \"Marie Curie\", \"birth_year\": 1867, \"fields\": [\"physics\", \"chemistry\"], \"nobel_prizes\": 2 } This makes AI outputs reliable for automation , database storage, and workflow pipelines where downstream systems expect specific data shapes. JSON vs. Other Formats JSON vs. XML: JSON is lighter and easier to read. XML is more verbose but supports schemas and namespaces. JSON vs. YAML: YAML is even more human-readable (no brackets/quotes) and is preferred for configuration files. JSON is preferred for data exchange. JSON vs. Markdown : Markdown is for formatted text humans will read. JSON is for structured data machines will process. Layman's Analogy JSON is like a standardized form. When you fill out a tax form, every field has a label (\"Name:\", \"Address:\", \"Income:\") and you put specific data in each field. JSON works the same way for computers — it's a universal \"form\" format that any system can read. Without it, AI systems would be like people shouting information at each other in different languages."},{"url":"topics/llm.html","title":"Large Language Model (LLM)","definition":"A Large Language Model is a type of AI model trained on massive amounts of text data that can understand, generate, and reason about human language.","headings":["What is an LLM?","How LLMs Work","Key Characteristics"],"text":"Large Language Model (LLM) A Large Language Model is a type of AI model trained on massive amounts of text data that can understand, generate, and reason about human language. What is an LLM? An LLM is a neural network (specifically, a Transformer ) with billions of parameters, trained by reading enormous amounts of text from books, websites, code, and other sources. Through this training, it learns the patterns and structure of language — grammar, facts, reasoning, and even some common sense. Examples of LLMs include Claude (Anthropic), GPT-4 (OpenAI), Gemini (Google), and Llama (Meta). These models power chatbots, writing assistants, code generators, and AI agents . How LLMs Work At their core, LLMs predict the next token (word or word-piece) in a sequence. Given \"The cat sat on the ___\", the model predicts \"mat\" is likely. By chaining these predictions together and being trained on trillions of tokens, LLMs can generate coherent paragraphs, solve math problems, write code, and carry on conversations. Key Characteristics Scale: Billions of parameters (Claude Opus has hundreds of billions) Context window : How much text they can process at once Training data : Trained on internet-scale text corpora Emergent abilities: Larger models develop capabilities not present in smaller ones Layman's Analogy An LLM is like someone who has read every book in every library in the world and can now have a conversation about any topic. They don't \"know\" things the way humans do — they've learned patterns about how words relate to each other. But the result looks remarkably like understanding."},{"url":"topics/machine-learning.html","title":"Machine Learning (ML)","definition":"Machine Learning is a subset of artificial intelligence where systems learn patterns from data and improve their performance without being explicitly programmed for each task.","headings":["What is Machine Learning?","Types of Machine Learning"],"text":"Machine Learning (ML) Machine Learning is a subset of artificial intelligence where systems learn patterns from data and improve their performance without being explicitly programmed for each task. What is Machine Learning? Traditional software follows rules written by humans: \"if X, then do Y.\" Machine learning flips this — you give the system examples (data) and it figures out the rules on its own. The more data it sees, the better it gets. ML powers recommendation engines (Netflix, Spotify), email spam filters, voice assistants, fraud detection, medical diagnosis tools, and large language models like Claude . Types of Machine Learning Supervised Learning: The system learns from labeled examples. \"Here are 10,000 emails labeled spam or not-spam — learn to tell the difference.\" Used for classification and prediction. Unsupervised Learning: The system finds patterns in unlabeled data. \"Here are customer purchase histories — find natural groupings.\" Used for clustering and anomaly detection. Reinforcement Learning: The system learns by trial and error, receiving rewards or penalties. \"Play this game a million times and figure out how to win.\" Used for robotics, game AI, and optimization. Layman's Analogy Machine learning is like teaching a child through examples rather than rules. Instead of explaining \"a cat has pointy ears, whiskers, and a tail,\" you show the child thousands of pictures of cats and dogs, and they learn to tell them apart on their own — even for cats they've never seen before."},{"url":"topics/markdown-format.html","title":"Markdown Format","definition":"Markdown is a lightweight text formatting language that uses simple symbols (like # for headings, * for bold) to structure documents. It's the preferred format for communicating with AI models because it survives preprocessing, is human-readable, and carries structural meaning that models have deeply learned.","headings":["What is Markdown?","Why Markdown Works So Well for AI","When to Use Markdown (vs. Other Formats)","Basic Markdown Syntax"],"text":"Markdown Format Markdown is a lightweight text formatting language that uses simple symbols (like # for headings, * for bold) to structure documents. It's the preferred format for communicating with AI models because it survives preprocessing, is human-readable, and carries structural meaning that models have deeply learned. What is Markdown? Markdown was created in 2004 by John Gruber as a way to write formatted text using plain characters. Instead of clicking buttons to make text bold (like in Word), you wrap it in asterisks: **bold** . Instead of using a menu to create a heading, you prefix with hashes: # Heading . It's used everywhere in tech: GitHub READMEs, Reddit posts, Stack Overflow answers, documentation sites, blog platforms, chat platforms (Slack, Discord), and even Wikipedia (which uses a wiki markup that converts cleanly to Markdown). All of these are prominent in AI training data . Why Markdown Works So Well for AI LLMs are not trained on raw HTML the way many people assume. Training data from web crawls (like Common Crawl) goes through heavy preprocessing: HTML tags get stripped, text gets extracted, the document is cleaned. The model rarely sees raw <h1> or <div> tags. This is exactly why Markdown is so powerful for AI: it survives preprocessing because it IS plain text with a few special characters. When the training pipeline strips HTML from a GitHub README, the Markdown formatting remains intact. The model has seen billions of examples of Markdown structure and learned what it means: # is a top-level header: The model treats text under bigger headers as more structurally important **bold** and *italic* carry semantic weight: The model learns these are emphasis markers signaling importance Lists signal discrete items: The model treats bulleted content as separate, parallel points Code blocks signal literal content: The model knows not to paraphrase text inside backticks When to Use Markdown (vs. Other Formats) The practical rule for working with Claude : Markdown for documents, RAG context, agent instructions (CLAUDE.md files, skills ), READMEs — anything humans will also read XML tags for production prompts with strict structure, multi-section prompts, or when the model must extract a specific section from a long input JSON when you need structured output or are feeding structured data Note: Anthropic states that the exact formatting is becoming less important as models become more capable. But for reliability in production, structure still helps. Basic Markdown Syntax # Heading 1 ## Heading 2 **bold text** *italic text* - Bullet point - Another point 1. Numbered list 2. Second item [Link text](https://example.com) `inline code` ``` code block ``` Example: Structured Prompt vs. Plain Text Plain text (worse): \"I want you to analyze this code for bugs and also suggest improvements and format the output nicely\" Markdown (better): ## Task Analyze the following code. ## Requirements 1. Identify any bugs 2. Suggest improvements 3. Rate overall quality (1-10) ## Output Format Use headings for each section."},{"url":"topics/mcp.html","title":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","definition":"The Model Context Protocol is an open standard that enables AI models to securely connect to external data sources and tools — giving them the ability to read databases, call APIs , browse files, and interact with software.","headings":["What is MCP?","How It Works","Adoption"],"text":"Model Context Protocol (MCP) The Model Context Protocol is an open standard that enables AI models to securely connect to external data sources and tools — giving them the ability to read databases, call APIs , browse files, and interact with software. What is MCP? MCP was introduced by Anthropic in late 2024 as an open-source protocol to solve a fundamental problem: AI models are powerful thinkers, but they're isolated — they can't see your files, access your databases, or use your tools without custom integration code for each one. MCP provides a universal standard for connecting AI to the outside world. Instead of building a custom connector for every tool, developers build one MCP server, and any MCP-compatible AI client can use it. Think of it as \"USB for AI\" — one standard plug that works everywhere. How It Works The protocol has three parts: MCP Hosts: AI applications (like Claude Code ) that want to access external tools MCP Clients: The protocol layer inside the host that manages connections MCP Servers: Lightweight programs that expose specific capabilities (a GitHub server, a database server, a file system server, etc.) Adoption By late 2025, MCP had over 97 million monthly SDK downloads, 10,000+ active servers in production, and support from virtually every major AI platform. In December 2025, Anthropic donated MCP governance to the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation, with backing from OpenAI, Google, Microsoft, and AWS. Layman's Analogy Imagine AI is a brilliant consultant locked in a room with no phone or internet. MCP is like giving them a phone with apps — now they can call your CRM, check your calendar, look up your files, and actually do things in the real world, not just talk about them."},{"url":"topics/meta-tags.html","title":"Meta Tags & OG Tags","definition":"Meta tags are invisible HTML elements in your page's <head> that tell search engines and social platforms what your page is about, how to display it, and how to index it.","headings":["What Are Meta Tags?","Essential Meta Tags","Open Graph (OG) Tags","Impact"],"text":"Meta Tags & OG Tags Meta tags are invisible HTML elements in your page's <head> that tell search engines and social platforms what your page is about, how to display it, and how to index it. What Are Meta Tags? Meta tags don't appear on the visible page — they live in the HTML source code. Search engines read them to understand your content, and social platforms read them to generate link previews. The two most important for SEO: Title tag ( <title> ): The clickable headline in Google search results. Keep it under 60 characters. This is the single most important on-page SEO element. Meta description ( <meta name=\"description\"> ): The 1-2 sentence summary shown below the title in search results. Keep it under 160 characters. Doesn't directly affect ranking, but directly affects whether people click. Essential Meta Tags <head> <!-- Page title (shows in browser tab + search results) --> <title>What is JSON? - AI Glossary</title> <!-- Description (shows below title in search results) --> <meta name=\"description\" content=\"JSON is a lightweight data format used for AI APIs, structured outputs, and automation configs.\"> <!-- Character encoding (always UTF-8) --> <meta charset=\"UTF-8\"> <!-- Mobile responsive (required for mobile search ranking) --> <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\"> <!-- Canonical URL (prevents duplicate content issues) --> <link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://yoursite.com/topics/json.html\"> </head> Open Graph (OG) Tags OG tags control how your page looks when shared on social media (LinkedIn, Twitter/X, Slack, Discord, Facebook). Without them, platforms show a generic link. With them, you get a rich preview with image, title, and description. <!-- Open Graph tags (social sharing) --> <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"What is JSON? - AI Glossary\"> <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"JSON explained for AI practitioners.\"> <meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://yoursite.com/images/json-preview.png\"> <meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://yoursite.com/topics/json.html\"> <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\"> <!-- Twitter/X card --> <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\"> <meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"What is JSON? - AI Glossary\"> <meta name=\"twitter:description\" content=\"JSON explained for AI practitioners.\"> <meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://yoursite.com/images/json-preview.png\"> Impact Your title tag and meta description are what people see before they decide to click on your page. A compelling title with a clear description can double your click-through rate from search results — even without improving your ranking position. OG tags determine whether your content looks professional or generic when shared in Slack, LinkedIn, or on social media. Layman's Analogy Meta tags are like the cover and back-cover blurb of a book. The title tag is the book title on the spine — what people see when scanning the shelf (search results). The meta description is the blurb — what convinces them to pick it up. OG tags are the book jacket design — what makes it look good when someone holds it up to show a friend (shares a link)."},{"url":"topics/mobile-apps.html","title":"Mobile Apps","definition":"A mobile application is software designed to run on smartphones and tablets (iOS and Android). Mobile apps are distributed through app stores and can access device hardware like the camera, GPS, accelerometer, and push notifications.","headings":["Web App vs Mobile App — Quick Decision"],"text":"Mobile Apps A mobile application is software designed to run on smartphones and tablets (iOS and Android). Mobile apps are distributed through app stores and can access device hardware like the camera, GPS, accelerometer, and push notifications. Native Mobile Apps Built specifically for one operating system using the platform's official language and tools. Native apps have the best performance and full access to device features. Platform Language IDE Examples iOS Swift (or Objective-C) Xcode Apple Maps, iMessage Android Kotlin (or Java) Android Studio Google Messages, Files Pros: Best performance, full hardware access, platform-specific UI conventions, app store optimization. Cons: Two separate codebases (double the work), need separate developer teams, slower to market. When to go native Performance-critical apps (games, video editors, AR) Deep hardware integration (real-time audio, advanced camera pipelines) Apps that must feel indistinguishable from OS-native experiences Cross-Platform Mobile Apps One codebase that compiles to both iOS and Android. In 2026, over 65% of new mobile apps use this approach. The performance gap with native has largely closed for standard applications. Leading Frameworks Framework Language By Market Share (2026) Flutter Dart Google ~46% React Native JavaScript / TypeScript Meta ~35% Flutter compiles Dart directly to native ARM machine code and uses its own rendering engine (Impeller) for pixel-perfect, consistent UI across platforms. Best for animation-heavy apps and consistent design. React Native renders actual native platform components, so the app feels genuinely native on each OS. Best when you have existing JavaScript/React developers or want to share code with a web app. Pros: Single codebase, 30-40% cost reduction, faster time to market, shared team. Cons: Slight overhead for deep hardware features, may need native modules for specialized functionality. AI tools can generate mobile app UI layouts, navigation patterns, and API integration code effectively in both Flutter and React Native. However, mobile apps require more human oversight than web apps due to app store review processes, device-specific testing, and platform guidelines that AI may not fully account for. AI is best used for generating components and screens, not entire production mobile apps from scratch. Web App vs Mobile App — Quick Decision Need a URL anyone can visit? → Web app Need push notifications, camera, or offline? → Mobile app Budget is tight, need both platforms? → Cross-platform (Flutter/React Native) Maximum performance, one platform? → Native Layman's Analogy Native development is like hiring a French chef for a French restaurant and a Japanese chef for a Japanese restaurant — each is perfect for their cuisine. Cross-platform is like hiring one talented chef who can cook both cuisines well from a single kitchen. Most diners won't notice the difference, but for a Michelin-star experience, the specialist wins."},{"url":"topics/multi-agent.html","title":"Multi-Agent Systems","definition":"A multi-agent system is an architecture where multiple specialized AI agents coordinate to solve complex problems — each agent handling a distinct role, communicating through defined protocols, much like a team of specialists collaborating on a project.","headings":["What are Multi-Agent Systems?","Orchestration Patterns","Key Standards (2026)","When to Use Multi-Agent (and When Not To)"],"text":"Multi-Agent Systems A multi-agent system is an architecture where multiple specialized AI agents coordinate to solve complex problems — each agent handling a distinct role, communicating through defined protocols, much like a team of specialists collaborating on a project. What are Multi-Agent Systems? A single agent can do a lot, but some problems benefit from division of labor. Instead of one agent juggling research, analysis, writing, and quality checking, you deploy several agents that each specialize in one domain and pass work between them. In 2026, 57% of organizations deploy multi-step agent workflows in production. Interest in multi-agent systems surged 1,445% in 2025 as organizations moved from single-agent prototypes to complex coordinated systems. Orchestration Patterns Supervisor/Worker A central supervisor agent receives the goal, breaks it into subtasks, routes each subtask to a specialist worker agent, and synthesizes the results. This is the most common production pattern — clear hierarchy, easy to debug, predictable coordination. Peer-to-Peer Agents communicate and collaborate directly without a central supervisor. Each agent can request help from others, negotiate task distribution, and contribute independently. More flexible but harder to debug and monitor. Hierarchical Multiple layers of supervisors managing teams of workers. Higher levels focus on planning and coordination; lower levels focus on execution. Scales well for enterprise-level complexity but adds latency at each coordination layer. Key Standards (2026) Google A2A (Agent-to-Agent): Open protocol for inter-agent communication. 150+ organizations using it in production as of April 2026. Defines how agents discover each other, exchange tasks, and report results. MCP (Model Context Protocol): Connects agents to tools and data sources. Complementary to A2A — A2A is for agents talking to agents, MCP is for agents talking to tools. Agent SDK : Anthropic's SDK supports multi-agent via handoffs — one agent can transfer control to another with full context. When to Use Multi-Agent (and When Not To) Use multi-agent when: The task requires distinct expertise domains, a single agent's context window is overwhelmed, or you need different security/permission levels for different subtasks. Don't use multi-agent when: A single well-tooled agent can handle the task. Most production agents are single agents with many tools . Multi-agent adds coordination overhead, debugging complexity, and latency. Important reality check: 40% of multi-agent pilots fail within 6 months of production deployment — overwhelmingly due to governance gaps, insufficient monitoring, and fragmented ownership. Start simple. Layman's Analogy A multi-agent system is like a hospital. You don't have one doctor doing everything — you have a triage nurse (router), a GP (generalist), specialists (cardiologist, radiologist), and a coordinator who makes sure everyone's notes end up in the right chart. Each person is excellent at their job, and the system works because they communicate clearly through structured protocols (patient charts, referral forms). Real-World Example: Automated Code Review A code review system with multiple specialized agents: Agent 1 (Security Reviewer) checks for vulnerabilities using security scanning tools. Agent 2 (Style Reviewer) enforces coding conventions and formatting. Agent 3 (Performance Reviewer) identifies bottlenecks and suggests optimizations. A Supervisor agent aggregates their findings into a unified review, resolves conflicting suggestions, and posts the combined feedback. Each specialist has different tools, different system prompts, and different expertise."},{"url":"topics/natural-language-processing.html","title":"Natural Language Processing (NLP)","definition":"Natural Language Processing is the branch of AI focused on enabling computers to understand, interpret, and generate human language — the technology that lets you talk to machines in plain English.","headings":["What is NLP?","NLP Tasks"],"text":"Natural Language Processing (NLP) Natural Language Processing is the branch of AI focused on enabling computers to understand, interpret, and generate human language — the technology that lets you talk to machines in plain English. What is NLP? Human language is messy, ambiguous, and context-dependent. \"Bank\" can mean a river bank or a financial bank. \"I saw her duck\" has two completely different meanings. NLP is the field that tackles these challenges — building systems that can read, understand, and produce natural language. Large language models like Claude represent the current state of the art in NLP — they can understand context, nuance, and even humor in ways that were impossible a few years ago. NLP Tasks Text generation: Writing essays, code, emails (what LLMs do) Sentiment analysis: Determining if text is positive/negative Translation: Converting between languages Summarization: Condensing long documents Named entity recognition: Identifying names, places, dates in text Question answering: Finding answers in documents Layman's Analogy NLP is like teaching a computer to be fluent in a human language. Before NLP, talking to a computer was like talking to someone who only understands exact commands (\"ERROR: command not found\"). With NLP, it's like talking to a fluent speaker who understands slang, context, and even sarcasm."},{"url":"topics/neural-network.html","title":"Neural Network","definition":"A neural network is a computing system inspired by the human brain, made up of interconnected nodes (\"neurons\") organized in layers that process information and learn patterns from data.","headings":["What is a Neural Network?","Structure"],"text":"Neural Network A neural network is a computing system inspired by the human brain, made up of interconnected nodes (\"neurons\") organized in layers that process information and learn patterns from data. What is a Neural Network? A neural network is the fundamental building block of modern AI . It consists of layers of mathematical functions (neurons) that transform input data into useful output. Each neuron receives inputs, applies weights to them, sums them up, and passes the result through an activation function. During training , the network adjusts its weights to minimize errors. After seeing millions of examples, the network learns to make accurate predictions on new, unseen data. Structure Input layer: Receives the raw data (pixels of an image, words of a sentence). Hidden layers: Process and transform the data. More layers = deeper network = deep learning . Output layer: Produces the final result (a classification, a prediction, generated text). Layman's Analogy Think of a neural network like a game of \"telephone\" with a twist. A message (data) enters at one end, passes through many people (neurons) who each slightly transform it based on what they've learned, and comes out the other end as an answer. During training, you tell the network whether the answer was right or wrong, and all the people adjust how they transform the message — getting better over time."},{"url":"topics/plugins.html","title":"Plugins","definition":"A plugin is a bundled, installable package that combines multiple Claude Code features — slash commands , subagents , MCP servers , and hooks — into one cohesive extension installed with a single command.","headings":["What is a Plugin?","How a Plugin Is Built","The Marketplace","When to Use a Plugin"],"text":"Plugins A plugin is a bundled, installable package that combines multiple Claude Code features — slash commands , subagents , MCP servers , and hooks — into one cohesive extension installed with a single command. What is a Plugin? Plugins are the highest-level way to extend Claude Code. Rather than copying a slash command here, configuring a subagent there, and wiring up hooks separately, a plugin packages all of it together. A user runs /plugin install pr-review and instantly gets every command, agent, and hook the plugin author bundled — fully configured. How a Plugin Is Built At its core a plugin is a folder with a .claude-plugin/plugin.json manifest declaring its name, version, and author. Around that manifest sit optional directories: commands/ , agents/ , skills/ , hooks/ , and MCP configuration. Claude Code reads the manifest, extracts the components, and registers them. The Marketplace Plugins are distributed through marketplaces — catalogs of available plugins. There is an official Anthropic-managed marketplace, community marketplaces, and private ones that companies run internally for their own tools and standards. Marketplaces can be added from a GitHub repository, a git URL, or a local path. When to Use a Plugin A single slash command or skill is fine for a personal, one-off workflow. Reach for a plugin when you need to bundle several features , share a setup with a team , or publish for distribution . Onboarding a new teammate becomes one install command instead of an afternoon of manual configuration. Layman's Analogy If a slash command is a single app, a plugin is an app bundle — and the marketplace is the app store. Instead of hunting down and configuring five separate tools, you install one package and everything works together out of the box, just like buying a productivity suite instead of assembling it piece by piece."},{"url":"topics/prompt.html","title":"Prompt","definition":"A prompt is the input you give to an AI model — the question, instruction, or context that tells the model what you want it to do.","headings":["What is a Prompt?","Prompt Engineering","Types of Prompts"],"text":"Prompt A prompt is the input you give to an AI model — the question, instruction, or context that tells the model what you want it to do. What is a Prompt? Every interaction with an LLM starts with a prompt. It can be as simple as \"What's the capital of France?\" or as complex as a multi-page document with instructions, examples, constraints, and context. The quality of your prompt directly determines the quality of the AI's response. Prompt Engineering \"Prompt engineering\" is the practice of crafting effective prompts to get the best results from AI models. Key techniques include: Be specific: \"Write a 200-word product description for a blue wool scarf targeting young professionals\" beats \"write about a scarf\" Give examples: Show the model what good output looks like (called \"few-shot prompting\") Assign a role: \"You are an expert nutritionist\" helps focus the response Break down complex tasks: \"First analyze, then recommend, then summarize\" Use structured formats : Markdown, XML tags, or numbered lists help organize complex prompts Types of Prompts System prompt: Background instructions that set the AI's behavior for an entire conversation (invisible to users in products). User prompt: The actual message from the human. Assistant prompt: The AI's response (which becomes context for the next turn). Layman's Analogy A prompt is like giving directions to a taxi driver. \"Take me somewhere nice\" might get an okay result. \"Take me to the Italian restaurant on 5th Street, avoiding the highway, and park on the left side\" will get exactly what you want. The more precise your directions, the better the journey."},{"url":"topics/rag.html","title":"RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)","definition":"RAG is a technique that improves LLM responses by first retrieving relevant information from external sources, then providing that information to the model as context before it generates an answer.","headings":["What is RAG?","How RAG Works","Why Use RAG?"],"text":"RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) RAG is a technique that improves LLM responses by first retrieving relevant information from external sources, then providing that information to the model as context before it generates an answer. What is RAG? Large language models have two fundamental limitations: their knowledge has a cutoff date (they don't know about recent events), and they can hallucinate (confidently state incorrect information). RAG solves both problems by giving the model access to up-to-date, authoritative sources at query time. The process: (1) User asks a question → (2) System searches a knowledge base for relevant documents → (3) Retrieved documents are added to the prompt → (4) LLM generates an answer grounded in those sources. How RAG Works Indexing: Documents are split into chunks and converted to embeddings (numerical representations), stored in a vector database Retrieval: When a query comes in, it's also converted to an embedding, and the system finds the most similar document chunks Generation: The retrieved chunks are passed to the LLM along with the question, and it generates an answer based on that specific information Why Use RAG? Answers are grounded in real sources (less hallucination ) Knowledge stays current without retraining the model Much cheaper than fine-tuning a model on your data You can cite sources for transparency Layman's Analogy RAG is like an open-book exam versus a closed-book exam. Without RAG, the AI answers purely from memory (which might be wrong or outdated). With RAG, the AI first looks up the relevant textbook pages, then answers using those sources — much more accurate and verifiable."},{"url":"topics/robots-txt.html","title":"robots.txt","definition":"robots.txt is a plain text file at the root of your website that tells search engine crawlers which pages they're allowed to visit and which they should skip.","headings":["What is robots.txt?","What It Looks Like","Common Patterns","2026 Context: AI Crawlers","Important: robots.txt is Not Security"],"text":"robots.txt robots.txt is a plain text file at the root of your website that tells search engine crawlers which pages they're allowed to visit and which they should skip. What is robots.txt? When a search engine's crawler (like Googlebot) first visits your site, the very first thing it checks is yoursite.com/robots.txt . This file contains rules about what the crawler can and cannot access. It's an honor system — crawlers voluntarily follow the rules, but it's not a security mechanism. Every website should have a robots.txt file, even if it just says \"everything is allowed.\" Without one, crawlers log a 404 error when they check — which is unnecessary noise. What It Looks Like # Allow all crawlers to access everything User-agent: * Allow: / # Point to the sitemap Sitemap: https://yoursite.com/sitemap.xml Common Patterns # Block all crawlers from a private folder User-agent: * Disallow: /admin/ Disallow: /private/ # Block a specific crawler (e.g., an AI training crawler) User-agent: GPTBot Disallow: / # Allow Google but block everything else User-agent: Googlebot Allow: / User-agent: * Disallow: / 2026 Context: AI Crawlers In 2026, robots.txt has gained new relevance because AI companies send their own crawlers to scrape web content for training data . Common AI crawler user-agents include: GPTBot — OpenAI ClaudeBot — Anthropic Google-Extended — Google AI training CCBot — Common Crawl You can block AI training crawlers while still allowing search engine crawlers to index your site. This is a separate decision from SEO — it's about whether you want your content used to train AI models. Important: robots.txt is Not Security robots.txt does not prevent access — it's a polite request. Anyone can still visit any URL directly. For actual security, use authentication or server-side access controls. robots.txt is for controlling crawler behavior, not hiding content. Layman's Analogy robots.txt is like the \"Staff Only\" sign on a door in a store. Well-behaved visitors (crawlers) will respect it and not enter. But it's not a lock — anyone who really wants to can push through. It sets boundaries for polite guests, not protection against trespassers."},{"url":"topics/routing.html","title":"Routing","definition":"Routing is an AI workflow pattern where one classification decision directs the input to the appropriate specialized path — like an air traffic controller sending each plane to the right runway based on a quick assessment.","headings":["What is Routing?","Why Routing Matters (2026)","Routing Approaches","Routing vs Agent Decision-Making"],"text":"Routing Routing is an AI workflow pattern where one classification decision directs the input to the appropriate specialized path — like an air traffic controller sending each plane to the right runway based on a quick assessment. What is Routing? A router evaluates an incoming request and dispatches it to the right handler. After that one decision, the path is fixed and predetermined. This makes routing a workflow pattern, not an agent pattern — the system makes one decision, then follows a defined track. This is one of Anthropic's five core building blocks for agentic systems, sitting between simple prompt chaining (zero decisions) and full agents (many decisions). Why Routing Matters (2026) Cost savings: Research by LMSYS (RouteLLM) shows intelligent routing can reduce costs by over 85% — sending simple queries to smaller, cheaper models Quality: Specialized prompts and models per category outperform one-size-fits-all Speed: Lightweight models handle easy tasks in milliseconds Scale: 37% of enterprises now use 5+ models in production — routing is how they manage this complexity Routing Approaches Keyword/rule-based: Cheapest and fastest. \"If message contains 'billing', route to billing handler.\" Least flexible. LLM-as-classifier: One LLM call to categorize the input, then route to the appropriate specialized path. Most common pattern. Local classifier models: Fine-tuned lightweight models (BERT-class) that detect task type — coding, math, analysis, creative writing, etc. Fast and cheap at inference. Confidence-based: If the model's confidence falls below a threshold, escalate to a larger/better model. Used for cost optimization. Consensus-based (2026): Send the same prompt to multiple models and aggregate their responses — emerging pattern for high-stakes decisions. Routing vs Agent Decision-Making A router makes one decision at the start. An agent makes many decisions throughout execution. If your problem needs exactly one classification step followed by specialized handling, use routing. If it needs ongoing judgment calls where each step depends on the previous result, use an agent . Layman's Analogy Routing is like calling a company's phone line. The automated menu asks \"Press 1 for billing, 2 for tech support, 3 for sales.\" One decision routes you to the right department, and from there the process is fixed. The menu didn't have a conversation with you — it just classified your need and sent you on your way. Real-World Example: Customer Support A customer support system receives a message. A router classifies it: billing / technical / sales / complaint. Each category routes to its own specialized prompt, model size, and response template. Technical queries go to a large model with code tools and documentation access. Simple billing questions (\"what's my balance?\") go to a smaller, cheaper model with database access. One decision, then a fixed path."},{"url":"topics/site-webmanifest.html","title":"site.webmanifest","definition":"A site.webmanifest (or web app manifest) is a JSON file that tells browsers metadata about your website — its name, icons, theme color, and how it should behave when installed as an app on a user's device.","headings":["What is a Web Manifest?","What It Looks Like","Key Fields","Is It Necessary?"],"text":"site.webmanifest A site.webmanifest (or web app manifest) is a JSON file that tells browsers metadata about your website — its name, icons, theme color, and how it should behave when installed as an app on a user's device. What is a Web Manifest? The manifest is a small JSON file, typically placed in your project root, that describes your site to browsers. It's what enables the \"Add to Home Screen\" feature on mobile devices — turning your website into something that looks and feels like a native app (called a Progressive Web App or PWA). You link to it from every HTML page: <link rel=\"manifest\" href=\"/site.webmanifest\"> What It Looks Like { \"name\": \"AI Glossary\", \"short_name\": \"AI Glossary\", \"description\": \"A comprehensive dictionary for understanding AI\", \"start_url\": \"/\", \"display\": \"standalone\", \"background_color\": \"#ffffff\", \"theme_color\": \"#0645ad\", \"icons\": [ { \"src\": \"/images/icon-192.png\", \"sizes\": \"192x192\", \"type\": \"image/png\" }, { \"src\": \"/images/icon-512.png\", \"sizes\": \"512x512\", \"type\": \"image/png\" } ] } Key Fields name — Full name of your site (shown on splash screens) short_name — Abbreviated name (shown under the app icon on home screens) start_url — Which page opens when the app is launched display — How the app looks: \"standalone\" (like a native app, no browser chrome), \"browser\" (normal tab), or \"fullscreen\" theme_color — Color of the browser address bar on mobile icons — App icons in multiple sizes (192px and 512px are the minimum) Is It Necessary? For SEO ranking: No. A web manifest has no direct impact on Google rankings. A missing manifest won't hurt your search position. For user experience and professionalism: Yes. It signals a modern, well-built site. Without it: Chrome on Android won't show an \"Add to Home Screen\" install prompt Your PWA won't have a splash screen or proper icon The browser address bar won't match your brand color Priority: This is a Tier 3 item — nice to have, not critical. Focus on meta tags , sitemap , and robots.txt first. Layman's Analogy A web manifest is like a business card for your website that it hands to phones and browsers. It says: \"Hi, my name is AI Glossary, my brand color is blue, here's my logo, and if you want to pin me to your home screen, open me like this.\" Without the business card, phones treat your site as just another anonymous web page."},{"url":"topics/sitemap-xml.html","title":"sitemap.xml","definition":"A sitemap.xml is a file that lists every page on your website, telling search engines exactly what content exists, when it was last updated, and how important each page is relative to others.","headings":["What is a Sitemap?","What It Looks Like","Key Fields","How to Use It"],"text":"sitemap.xml A sitemap.xml is a file that lists every page on your website, telling search engines exactly what content exists, when it was last updated, and how important each page is relative to others. What is a Sitemap? A sitemap is a roadmap of your website written in XML format. You place it in your site's root directory (e.g., yoursite.com/sitemap.xml ). Search engine crawlers (Googlebot, Bingbot) read this file to discover and understand the structure of your site. Without a sitemap, search engines rely solely on following links between pages to discover content. If a page isn't linked from anywhere, it may never be found. A sitemap guarantees that every page is on the radar. What It Looks Like <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?> <urlset xmlns=\"http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9\"> <url> <loc>https://yoursite.com/</loc> <lastmod>2026-05-16</lastmod> <priority>1.0</priority> </url> <url> <loc>https://yoursite.com/topics/claude.html</loc> <lastmod>2026-05-16</lastmod> <priority>0.8</priority> </url> <!-- ... one entry per page ... --> </urlset> Key Fields <loc> — The full URL of the page (required) <lastmod> — When the page was last updated (helps Google prioritize recrawling) <priority> — Relative importance from 0.0 to 1.0 (hint to crawlers, not a ranking factor) <changefreq> — How often the page changes (daily, weekly, monthly) — largely ignored by Google in 2026 How to Use It Create the file and place it at your site's root Reference it in your robots.txt : Sitemap: https://yoursite.com/sitemap.xml Submit it in Google Search Console → Sitemaps → enter the URL → Submit Layman's Analogy A sitemap is like the table of contents of a book. Without it, a reader (search engine) would have to flip through every page to find what's there. With a table of contents, they can immediately see every chapter (page), what it's called (URL), and jump straight to it. You also tell them which chapters were recently revised."},{"url":"topics/slash-commands.html","title":"Slash Commands","definition":"Slash commands are typed shortcuts — starting with / — that control Claude Code's behavior during an interactive session, from built-in actions like /clear to custom workflows you define yourself.","headings":["What are Slash Commands?","The Four Types","Custom Commands Are Now Skills","Example: the /pr Command"],"text":"Slash Commands Slash commands are typed shortcuts — starting with / — that control Claude Code's behavior during an interactive session, from built-in actions like /clear to custom workflows you define yourself. What are Slash Commands? Inside a Claude Code session, typing / opens a menu of commands. They are the quickest way to steer a session — switching models, clearing context, reviewing changes, or kicking off a multi-step workflow — without writing a full prompt . There are 60+ built-in commands ; type / followed by any letters to filter the list. The Four Types Not every slash command is the same kind of thing. They come from four sources: Type Examples Where it comes from Built-in /help , /clear , /model , /init , /rewind Ship with Claude Code Skills /commit , /optimize , /pr User-defined SKILL.md files Plugin commands /review-pr , /deploy Installed plugins MCP prompts /mcp__github__list_prs MCP servers Custom Commands Are Now Skills Custom slash commands have been merged into skills . Older files in .claude/commands/ still work, but the recommended way to create a new command is a skill: a folder with a SKILL.md file whose name becomes the /command-name . Skills can do more than legacy commands — bundle scripts and templates, accept arguments, and even be auto-invoked by Claude when relevant. Example: the /pr Command A custom /pr command can walk through an entire pull-request checklist — formatting code, running tests, reviewing the diff, and writing a conventional commit message — all from two keystrokes: The /pr command running: Claude builds a todo list and works through the PR preparation steps. Layman's Analogy Slash commands are like the speed-dial buttons on a phone. Instead of typing out a full request every time, you press one shortcut and a whole routine happens. Some buttons are pre-set by the manufacturer (built-in), and some you program yourself for the calls you make often (skills)."},{"url":"topics/static-websites.html","title":"Static Websites","definition":"A static website is a collection of pre-built files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) served directly to the browser without any server-side processing. The content is \"static\" — it doesn't change per request. What you write is exactly what visitors see.","headings":["The Three Languages of Static Websites","What Static Sites Are Good For","What Static Sites Cannot Do","How to Deploy a Static Site"],"text":"Static Websites A static website is a collection of pre-built files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) served directly to the browser without any server-side processing. The content is \"static\" — it doesn't change per request. What you write is exactly what visitors see. The Three Languages of Static Websites Every static website is built from three core technologies that work together: HTML (Structure) HyperText Markup Language defines what is on the page — headings, paragraphs, links, images, lists. It's the skeleton of every web page. Without HTML, there is no web page. <h1>Welcome to My Site</h1> <p>This is a paragraph of text.</p> <a href=\"about.html\">Learn more</a> CSS (Appearance) Cascading Style Sheets control how things look — colors, fonts, spacing, layout, responsive design. CSS transforms plain HTML structure into visually appealing pages. body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; } h1 { color: #2c3e50; } JavaScript (Behavior) JavaScript adds interactivity — clicking buttons, showing/hiding elements, form validation, animations, fetching data. Not required for basic sites, but essential for dynamic behavior. document.querySelector('button').addEventListener('click', () => { alert('You clicked the button!'); }); Static websites are the single easiest type of software for AI to generate. A single prompt like \"create a portfolio website with a dark theme and project gallery\" produces complete, working HTML/CSS/JS that you can immediately deploy. AI tools like Claude, v0, and Bolt.new generate pixel-perfect static sites in seconds. This is the best starting point for anyone learning to build with AI. What Static Sites Are Good For Portfolios and resumes — showcase your work Blogs and documentation — publish information Landing pages — promote a product or idea Business websites — basic company presence Glossaries and wikis — like this site you're reading What Static Sites Cannot Do User accounts and login systems Saving data to a database Sending emails or processing payments Personalized content per user For these, you need a full-stack application . How to Deploy a Static Site Static sites are free to host because they require no server processing. The most common option: Write your HTML/CSS/JS files Push to a Git repository Enable GitHub Pages — your site is live at username.github.io/repo-name Layman's Analogy A static website is like a printed poster pinned to a bulletin board. Everyone who walks by sees the same poster. If you want to update it, you print a new one and pin it up. A full-stack app is like a counter with a person behind it — they look up your name, pull your file, and give you personalized information."},{"url":"topics/structured-data.html","title":"Structured Data (JSON-LD / Schema.org)","definition":"Structured data is machine-readable code (in JSON-LD format) embedded in your web pages that explicitly tells search engines and AI systems what your content is — not just what it says, but what it means .","headings":["What is Structured Data?","What It Looks Like","Why It Matters in 2026","Most Important Schema Types","Why JSON-LD?"],"text":"Structured Data (JSON-LD / Schema.org) Structured data is machine-readable code (in JSON-LD format) embedded in your web pages that explicitly tells search engines and AI systems what your content is — not just what it says, but what it means . What is Structured Data? When Google reads your page, it sees text. It can guess that \"Marie Curie\" is a person and \"1867\" is a year, but it might be wrong. Structured data removes the guessing — you explicitly declare: \"This page is about a Person. Her name is Marie Curie. She was born in 1867. She won 2 Nobel Prizes.\" The vocabulary comes from Schema.org (a standard maintained by Google, Microsoft, Yahoo, and Yandex). The format is JSON-LD — a block of JSON inside a <script> tag that doesn't affect the visible page. What It Looks Like <script type=\"application/ld+json\"> { \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"Article\", \"headline\": \"What is JSON?\", \"description\": \"JSON is a lightweight data format for AI APIs.\", \"author\": { \"@type\": \"Person\", \"name\": \"Your Name\" }, \"datePublished\": \"2026-05-16\", \"publisher\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"AI Glossary\" } } </script> Why It Matters in 2026 Structured data has become dramatically more important because of AI search: 35% higher click-through rates — Pages with structured data earn \"rich results\" (star ratings, FAQ dropdowns, images) in Google that stand out visually 2.3x more likely to appear in AI Overviews — Google's AI answers and Perplexity cite structured pages more often AI systems parse it reliably — LLMs can extract JSON-LD data more accurately than unstructured prose Knowledge Graph recognition — Google's entity database uses structured data to understand who and what you are Most Important Schema Types Organization — Who you are as a company/entity Article / BlogPosting — Content pages (like this glossary) FAQPage — Question-and-answer content Product — E-commerce product pages LocalBusiness — Physical business locations Why JSON-LD? Google recommends JSON-LD over alternatives (Microdata, RDFa) because it lives in a separate <script> block, doesn't mix with your HTML, is easier to maintain, and is easier for AI crawlers to parse. Layman's Analogy Regular HTML is like writing a résumé as a free-form essay. Structured data is like filling out a standardized form: Name ___, Job Title ___, Years of Experience ___. Both contain the same information, but the form lets a computer instantly extract and compare every field. That's what structured data does for search engines — turns your prose into a standardized form they can read instantly."},{"url":"topics/subagents.html","title":"Subagents","definition":"A subagent is a specialized AI assistant that Claude Code delegates a task to — running in its own context window , with its own instructions and its own restricted set of tools.","headings":["What is a Subagent?","Why Delegate?","Built-in and Custom Subagents","Subagents vs. Multi-Agent Systems"],"text":"Subagents A subagent is a specialized AI assistant that Claude Code delegates a task to — running in its own context window , with its own instructions and its own restricted set of tools. What is a Subagent? When the main Claude Code session hits a focused job — reviewing code, hunting a bug, writing tests — it can hand that job to a subagent instead of doing it inline. The subagent starts with a clean slate , receives only the context it needs, does the work, and returns a distilled result. The main conversation never sees the subagent's intermediate steps. Why Delegate? Benefit What it means Context preservation The subagent's exploration doesn't clutter the main conversation's context window Specialized expertise A custom system prompt tunes it for one domain — security, testing, debugging Tool control A read-only reviewer can be denied write access entirely Parallelism Several subagents can work on different parts of a task at once Built-in and Custom Subagents Claude Code ships with built-in subagents like Explore (fast, read-only codebase search), Plan (research for plan mode), and general-purpose (complex multi-step work). You can also define your own as Markdown files in .claude/agents/ , each with YAML frontmatter setting its name, description, allowed tools, and model. The /agents slash command creates and manages them interactively. Subagents vs. Multi-Agent Systems Subagents are one form of a multi-agent system . The defining trait is the parent–child relationship : a subagent is a delegated subtask that returns a result to its parent. This differs from \"agent teams,\" where independent agents work side by side and message each other directly. Subagents also cannot spawn their own subagents — delegation is one level deep. Layman's Analogy A subagent is like a specialist a general contractor calls in. The contractor doesn't rewire the house personally — they bring in an electrician, hand over just the relevant blueprints, and get back a finished job. The electrician's mess of tools and trips to the truck never crowds the contractor's own workspace."},{"url":"topics/technical-seo.html","title":"Technical SEO","definition":"Technical SEO is the practice of optimizing the infrastructure of a website so that search engines (and increasingly, AI systems) can efficiently crawl, index, understand, and surface your content.","headings":["Why Technical SEO Matters","The Essentials, Ranked by Impact","What Your Project Root Should Look Like"],"text":"Technical SEO Technical SEO is the practice of optimizing the infrastructure of a website so that search engines (and increasingly, AI systems) can efficiently crawl, index, understand, and surface your content. Why Technical SEO Matters You can write the best content in the world, but if search engines can't find it, read it, or trust it, nobody sees it. Technical SEO is the foundation layer — get it wrong, and nothing else matters. Get it right, and every piece of content you create has the best possible chance of being discovered. In 2026, Technical SEO has expanded beyond just Google. AI search engines — Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude — now crawl and cite web content. These systems rely on structured, well-organized sites with clear signals about what each page is and why it matters. The same technical foundations that help Google also help AI systems surface your content. The Essentials, Ranked by Impact Every item below has its own detailed topic page. They're listed here in order of importance: Tier 1 — Non-Negotiable (must have) Meta Tags & OG Tags The single most impactful thing you control. Title tags and meta descriptions determine how your pages appear in search results. Open Graph tags control how links look when shared on social media. Every page needs them. sitemap.xml A file that lists every page on your site, telling search engines exactly what exists and when it was last updated. Submit it to Google Search Console so Google starts crawling immediately. robots.txt A file that tells search engine crawlers which parts of your site they're allowed (or not allowed) to access. Controls what gets indexed. HTTPS / SSL Every page must load over HTTPS (encrypted connection). It's been a Google ranking signal since 2014 and is now a baseline requirement — sites on HTTP get \"Not Secure\" browser warnings and ranking penalties. If you use GitHub Pages , HTTPS is automatic. Google Search Console Free tool that shows you exactly how Google sees your site — what's indexed, what's broken, which searches lead to your pages. Setup takes 10 minutes and is essential for monitoring everything else on this list. Tier 2 — Important (strong competitive advantage) Structured Data (JSON-LD / Schema.org) Machine-readable metadata that tells Google what your content is (an article, a FAQ, a product). Pages with structured data earn 35% higher click-through rates and are 2.3x more likely to appear in AI Overviews. Canonical URLs Tells search engines which version of a page is the \"master copy\" when multiple URLs lead to the same content. Prevents duplicate content penalties. Core Web Vitals (Page Speed) Google's performance metrics: LCP (loading speed) < 2.5s, INP (interactivity) < 200ms, CLS (visual stability) < 0.1. Fast sites rank higher and keep users engaged. Tier 3 — Good to Have (polish and professionalism) site.webmanifest A JSON file that describes your site to browsers — name, icons, theme color. Enables \"Add to Home Screen\" on mobile. Not critical for SEO ranking, but signals a modern, professional site. Favicon The small icon in browser tabs. Google shows favicons in mobile search results. A missing favicon looks unprofessional and slightly hurts trust signals. What Your Project Root Should Look Like your-project/ ├── index.html ← Main page ├── sitemap.xml ← For search engines ├── robots.txt ← Crawl rules ├── site.webmanifest ← Browser metadata ├── favicon.ico ← Tab icon ├── css/ ├── topics/ └── images/ Layman's Analogy Technical SEO is like building codes for a house. Your house might have beautiful interior design (great content), but if it doesn't have a street address (sitemap), a visible front door (meta tags), building permits on file (Search Console), locks (HTTPS), and a sign saying \"open\" (robots.txt) — visitors and inspectors can't find it, enter it, or trust it. Technical SEO makes sure your \"house\" is on the map and meets all the codes."},{"url":"topics/temperature.html","title":"Temperature","definition":"Temperature is a setting that controls how random or creative an LLM's responses are — low temperature means predictable and focused, high temperature means varied and creative.","headings":["What is Temperature?","When to Adjust Temperature"],"text":"Temperature Temperature is a setting that controls how random or creative an LLM's responses are — low temperature means predictable and focused, high temperature means varied and creative. What is Temperature? When an LLM generates text, it predicts the probability of each possible next token . Temperature controls how those probabilities are used: Temperature 0: Always picks the most likely token. Deterministic, consistent, but potentially boring or repetitive. Temperature 0.5-0.7: Mostly picks likely tokens but allows some variation. Good balance for most tasks. Temperature 1.0+: More willing to pick less likely tokens. More creative and surprising, but may be less coherent. When to Adjust Temperature Low temperature (0-0.3): Use for factual questions, code generation, data extraction, math — tasks where there's one correct answer. Medium temperature (0.5-0.7): Use for general conversation, writing, summarization. High temperature (0.8-1.0): Use for brainstorming, creative writing, poetry — tasks where variety is valued. Layman's Analogy Temperature is like a \"creativity dial.\" At zero, the AI is like a strict accountant — always giving the safest, most expected answer. Turn it up, and it becomes more like a jazz musician — improvising, taking risks, sometimes brilliant, sometimes off-key. You adjust based on whether you need precision or inspiration."},{"url":"topics/token.html","title":"Token","definition":"A token is the basic unit of text that an LLM processes — roughly equivalent to a word or word-piece. It's how AI \"reads\" and \"writes\" language.","headings":["What is a Token?","Why Tokens Matter","Token Counts"],"text":"Token A token is the basic unit of text that an LLM processes — roughly equivalent to a word or word-piece. It's how AI \"reads\" and \"writes\" language. What is a Token? Large language models don't read individual letters or whole sentences — they break text into tokens. A token is typically about 3-4 characters or roughly ¾ of a word. Common words like \"the\" are one token; longer or rarer words get split into multiple tokens. For example, \"understanding\" might be split into \"under\" + \"standing.\" Why Tokens Matter Cost: AI APIs charge per token processed. More tokens = more expensive. Context window : Models have a maximum number of tokens they can handle at once (e.g., 200K tokens for Claude). Speed: Models generate one token at a time. More tokens = slower response. Token Counts As a rough guide: 1 token ≈ 4 characters in English. So 100 tokens ≈ 75 words. A full page of text is about 300-400 tokens. A novel is about 100,000 tokens. Claude's context window of 200K tokens means it can process roughly the equivalent of two full novels at once. Layman's Analogy Tokens are like puzzle pieces that make up language. Instead of reading letter by letter (too slow) or sentence by sentence (too big), the AI reads in word-sized chunks. It's like how you read — you don't look at each letter in \"cat,\" you see the whole word as one unit."},{"url":"topics/tool-use.html","title":"Tool Use (Function Calling)","definition":"Tool use (also called function calling) is the mechanism that allows an LLM to take actions beyond generating text — by outputting structured requests that a system executes on its behalf, returning real-world results back to the model.","headings":["What is Tool Use?","The Tool-Use Loop","How It Works (Technical)","Best Practices (2026)"],"text":"Tool Use (Function Calling) Tool use (also called function calling) is the mechanism that allows an LLM to take actions beyond generating text — by outputting structured requests that a system executes on its behalf, returning real-world results back to the model. What is Tool Use? By default, an LLM is text-in, text-out. It can reason brilliantly, but it cannot check today's weather, query a database, or send an email. Tool use bridges this gap: the model outputs a structured function call (name + arguments), the host system executes it, and feeds the result back. The model never runs code itself — it requests actions and interprets results. This is the foundational mechanism that makes AI agents possible. Without tools, an LLM can only advise. With tools, it can act. The Tool-Use Loop The standard architecture powering all AI agents is a loop: Observe: The model receives context (user request, previous results) Think: The model reasons about what to do next Call Tool: The model outputs a structured function call Get Result: The system executes the call and returns the result Decide: The model evaluates the result — either calls another tool or responds to the user This loop repeats until the model decides the task is complete. A single user request might trigger 1 tool call or 50, depending on complexity. How It Works (Technical) Tool definitions are provided in the system prompt — each tool has a name, description, and parameter schema (usually JSON ) The model decides to call a tool instead of generating a text response The system executes the function (API call, database query, file operation) The result is returned to the model as a new message The model continues reasoning with the new information Best Practices (2026) Start with one tool: Build an agent that calls a single function reliably before adding more Write excellent descriptions: Tool descriptions are the highest-leverage optimization. Instead of \"Search the web,\" use \"Search the web for current information. Use when the user asks about recent events or data that may have changed since training.\" Dynamic loading at scale: 50+ tool definitions consume ~55,000 tokens in the system prompt. The solution is loading a relevant subset per task via semantic search over tool descriptions Set iteration limits: Always set max_iterations (5–10) to prevent infinite loops Typed error signals: Return structured errors, not empty results. \"Rate limited, retry in 3s\" is far more useful than an empty array Log everything: Every function call with user context, arguments, result, and timestamp — essential for debugging and compliance Layman's Analogy An LLM without tools is like a brilliant advisor locked in a room with no phone or computer. They can give amazing analysis — but cannot check today's stock price, send an email, or look up a customer record. Tool use gives them a phone, a computer, and a filing cabinet. They still don't physically do the work — they write a request on a slip of paper, slide it under the door, and someone on the other side executes it and slides the answer back. Real-World Example: Claude Code Claude Code uses tool calls for everything: Read File (to understand code), Edit File (to make changes), Run Bash (to execute tests), Search (to find relevant files). Each action is a structured tool call — Claude never directly touches the filesystem. It requests, the system executes, and the result comes back for Claude to reason about next."},{"url":"topics/training-data.html","title":"Training Data","definition":"Training data is the collection of examples that an AI model learns from during the machine learning process — the \"textbooks\" the model studies.","headings":["What is Training Data?","Key Concepts"],"text":"Training Data Training data is the collection of examples that an AI model learns from during the machine learning process — the \"textbooks\" the model studies. What is Training Data? Every AI model is shaped by its training data. For large language models , training data includes books, websites, academic papers, code repositories, and other text sources — often trillions of tokens worth. The model learns language patterns, facts, reasoning, and style from this data. The quality, diversity, and size of training data directly determines the model's capabilities and limitations. A model trained only on English text won't understand French. A model never shown code won't know how to program. Key Concepts Knowledge cutoff: The model only knows what was in its training data. Claude's knowledge has a cutoff date after which it doesn't know about events. Bias: If training data over-represents certain viewpoints, the model will too. Data quality: \"Garbage in, garbage out\" — poor quality data leads to poor model performance. Scale: Modern LLMs are trained on multiple terabytes of text data. Layman's Analogy Training data is to AI what life experience is to a person. A person raised reading only science fiction would have a very different worldview than someone who read everything. Similarly, an AI model's \"personality\" and knowledge come entirely from what it was trained on. The better and more diverse the training data, the more capable and balanced the model."},{"url":"topics/transformer.html","title":"Transformer","definition":"The Transformer is a neural network architecture introduced in 2017 that revolutionized AI by enabling models to process entire sequences of data in parallel using a mechanism called \"attention.\"","headings":["What is a Transformer?","The Key Innovation: Attention","Why It Matters"],"text":"Transformer The Transformer is a neural network architecture introduced in 2017 that revolutionized AI by enabling models to process entire sequences of data in parallel using a mechanism called \"attention.\" What is a Transformer? The Transformer is the architecture behind all modern large language models — including Claude , GPT, Gemini, and Llama. It was introduced in the 2017 paper \"Attention Is All You Need\" by researchers at Google. Before Transformers, AI models processed text one word at a time (sequentially). Transformers process all words simultaneously, making them much faster to train and much better at understanding relationships between distant parts of a text. The Key Innovation: Attention The \"attention mechanism\" lets the model look at all parts of the input at once and decide which parts are most relevant to each other. In the sentence \"The cat sat on the mat because it was tired,\" attention helps the model understand that \"it\" refers to \"cat\" — even though other words sit between them. Why It Matters Transformers enabled the scaling revolution in AI. Because they process data in parallel, they can be trained on massive datasets using thousands of GPUs simultaneously. This scalability is what made billion-parameter models like Claude possible. Every major AI breakthrough since 2017 has been built on the Transformer architecture. Layman's Analogy Imagine reading a mystery novel. Older AI was like reading one word at a time, forgetting earlier pages. A Transformer is like being able to see every page simultaneously — spotting that a clue on page 3 connects to the revelation on page 200. The \"attention\" mechanism is your finger connecting those two passages with a line."},{"url":"topics/web-apps.html","title":"Web Apps","definition":"A web application is software that runs in a web browser. Users access it via a URL — no installation required. Web apps range from simple static pages (like this glossary) to complex full-stack systems (like Gmail or Notion).","headings":["Two Types of Web Apps","When to Use a Web App"],"text":"Web Apps A web application is software that runs in a web browser. Users access it via a URL — no installation required. Web apps range from simple static pages (like this glossary) to complex full-stack systems (like Gmail or Notion). Two Types of Web Apps Static Websites — Frontend Only Pre-built HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files served directly to the browser. No server-side processing happens per request. The content is the same for every visitor unless JavaScript modifies it client-side. Examples: Blogs, portfolios, documentation, landing pages, this glossary Hosting: Free on GitHub Pages , Netlify, Cloudflare Pages Technologies: HTML, CSS, JavaScript Full-Stack Applications — Frontend + Backend A complete system with a user-facing interface, server logic, and a database. Can handle user accounts, payments, real-time data, and personalized experiences. Examples: Gmail, Spotify Web, Amazon, ChatGPT Hosting: Requires a server (Vercel, AWS, Railway, etc.) Technologies: React/Vue + Node.js/Python + PostgreSQL/MongoDB When to Use a Web App You want instant access — no download or installation You need to work across devices (phone, tablet, desktop) You want easy updates (change the code, users get the new version immediately) You don't need deep hardware access (camera, GPS, file system) Web apps are the strongest category for AI-assisted development. AI can generate complete static websites from a single description, and for full-stack apps, AI handles UI components, API routes, and database schemas with high accuracy. Over 90% of professional developers use AI assistance for web development in 2026. Layman's Analogy A web app is like a restaurant you can walk into without a reservation. A static website is the menu posted on the window — same for everyone. A full-stack app is the full dining experience — you sit down, order, pay, and the kitchen (server) prepares something personalized for you."},{"url":"topics/workflow.html","title":"Workflow","definition":"A workflow is a predetermined sequence of steps where you define the path and the LLM executes each step — but never decides what to do next. This contrasts with AI agents , where the model dynamically chooses its own path.","headings":["What is a Workflow?","Workflow vs Agent: The Key Distinction","Anthropic's Workflow Building Blocks"],"text":"Workflow A workflow is a predetermined sequence of steps where you define the path and the LLM executes each step — but never decides what to do next. This contrasts with AI agents , where the model dynamically chooses its own path. What is a Workflow? A workflow is any repeatable process with defined steps. In traditional business: \"receive order → process payment → ship product → send confirmation email.\" In AI: \"receive user request → search knowledge base → generate draft → check for accuracy → deliver response.\" The critical insight: in a workflow, the code defines what happens at each step. The LLM does the work, but never makes strategic decisions about what to do next. The path is hardcoded in your script. Workflow vs Agent: The Key Distinction Workflow Agent Who decides the next step? You (the developer) The model Predictability High — same path every time Variable — depends on what model finds Cost Predictable, usually lower Variable, can spiral Flexibility Rigid — change the path, change the code Handles edge cases you didn't plan for Best for Repetitive, well-defined tasks Open-ended, novel tasks Anthropic's strong advice: start with a single prompt, then a workflow, and only reach for an agent when the task genuinely needs the LLM to make decisions about what to do next. Most production \"AI agents\" are actually workflows — and that is correct. Anthropic's Workflow Building Blocks Anthropic identifies five composable patterns for building AI workflows, from simplest to most complex: 1. Prompt Chaining The simplest pattern: a fixed sequence of LLM calls where the output of one becomes the input to the next. Each step has its own specialized prompt. Like a relay race — each runner (prompt) handles their leg, then passes the baton. 2. Routing One LLM call classifies the input, then directs it to the appropriate specialized path. After that one decision, the path is fixed. See the full Routing topic for details. 3. Parallelization Fan-out: send the same input to multiple LLMs simultaneously (e.g., generate 3 different drafts). Fan-in: aggregate the results (e.g., pick the best one, or combine insights from all three). Trades cost for speed and quality. 4. Orchestrator-Workers One LLM breaks a task into subtasks and delegates them — but the workers execute fixed, predetermined operations. The orchestrator adds a light layer of dynamic task decomposition while keeping execution predictable. 5. Evaluator-Optimizer A generate-then-evaluate loop: one LLM produces output, another evaluates it against criteria, and the first revises based on feedback. The iteration pattern is predetermined (generate → evaluate → revise), even though the content evolves. Layman's Analogy A workflow is like an assembly line in a factory. Each station does one specific job, and the product moves from station to station until it's complete. The assembly line never decides to skip a station or add a new one — that's predetermined by the factory manager. AI workflows are the same: the \"stations\" are AI operations, and the \"product\" is information being processed and refined at each step. Real-World Example: E-Commerce Review Pipeline An online shop gets 50 customer reviews daily in mixed languages and wants a summary report: Step 1: Take each review → LLM prompt: \"Translate this to English.\" → Output: English review. Step 2: Take English review → LLM prompt: \"Classify as positive, neutral, or negative.\" → Output: a label. Step 3: Send all 50 classified reviews → LLM prompt: \"Write a one-paragraph summary highlighting the top three complaints and top three praises.\" → Output: daily report. Each step is hardcoded. The code says \"first translate, then classify, then summarize.\" The LLM never decides what to do next — it just executes the task in front of it. This is a workflow, not an agent."},{"url":"topics/xml.html","title":"XML (in AI Prompting)","definition":"In AI context, XML tags are paired delimiters like <instructions>...</instructions> used to structure prompts with clear, unambiguous boundaries that the model can reliably identify and follow.","headings":["Why XML Tags for AI Prompts?","When to Use XML Tags","Practical Examples","XML vs. Markdown in Prompts","Common Tag Conventions"],"text":"XML (in AI Prompting) In AI context, XML tags are paired delimiters like <instructions>...</instructions> used to structure prompts with clear, unambiguous boundaries that the model can reliably identify and follow. Why XML Tags for AI Prompts? Claude has been specifically fine-tuned to pay attention to XML tag structure in prompts. Anthropic's official guidance: \"Mark up your prompts with XML tags. Claude has been specifically tuned to pay special attention to your structure.\" The technical reason: tokenization can cause problems when you rely on whitespace and indentation (the model might not \"see\" the spaces the same way you do). XML tags provide multi-line certainty — they are clear delimiters that unambiguously mark where a section begins and ends, regardless of how the tokenizer splits the text. When to Use XML Tags Production prompts with strict structure requirements Multi-section prompts where the model must distinguish between instructions, context, examples, and user input Extraction tasks where you need the model to isolate a specific section from a long input System prompts for applications built on the Claude API Practical Examples <system> You are a customer support agent for Acme Corp. Only answer questions about our products. </system> <context> {{retrieved product documentation from RAG}} </context> <user_message> {{the customer's actual question}} </user_message> <instructions> Answer the customer's question using only the context provided. If the answer isn't in the context, say \"I'll escalate this to a human agent.\" </instructions> XML vs. Markdown in Prompts XML tags are the stronger structural signal for Claude. Use them to separate sections . Markdown is better for formatting content within sections (headings, lists, bold text). Best practice: use XML to define the architecture of your prompt, and Markdown for the readable content inside. They complement each other. Note: As models become more capable, the exact formatting matters less. But for production systems where reliability is critical, XML structure still gives the most consistent results. Common Tag Conventions <instructions> — What the model should do <context> — Background information or retrieved documents <examples> — Few-shot examples <input> — The actual data to process <output_format> — How to structure the response <constraints> — Rules or limitations Layman's Analogy XML tags are like labeled folders on a desk. If you give someone a stack of loose papers, they have to guess what's what. But if you put instructions in a folder labeled \"INSTRUCTIONS,\" context in a folder labeled \"CONTEXT,\" and the question in a folder labeled \"QUESTION\" — they know exactly where to look for each piece. XML tags are those folder labels for AI."}];
